{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22195","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22195","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22195","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dar","familyName":"Gilboa","name":"Gilboa, Dar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddhartha","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Siddhartha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Or","familyName":"Sattath","name":"Sattath, Or","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decentralized Consensus from Quantum Proof of Position"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:44:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:09:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T03:01:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:19:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T22:08:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-08T00:17:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We construct an energy efficient consensus protocol with geospatial position as an external resource by using quantum computers. Economic resources of some form are essential in decentralized consensus protocols in order to prevent attacks in which malicious parties costlessly generate fake identities.\n The resulting protocol, Quantum Proof of Position (QPoP), improves on existing classical mechanisms: it dispenses with the notorious electricity consumption of Proof-of-Work, and, because its resource is external to the protocol, with the wealth concentration of Proof-of-Stake. QPoP also has two properties that, to our knowledge, no other permissionless Sybil resistance mechanism possesses. First, its Sybil resistance is non-rival: position verification occupies a quantum computer only briefly, so a single device can secure any number of chains at once, each enjoying its full benefit. Second, the protocol generates geographic decentralization, because its resource cannot be agglomerated: a participant's power is the number of distinct locations at which it operates a quantum computer, and a second computer at an occupied location adds nothing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22195","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:14Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:05:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22057","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22057","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22057","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonardo","familyName":"Zambrano","name":"Zambrano, Leonardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum tomography for non-iid sources"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:06:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:01:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-15T10:45:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-16T00:40:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum state and process tomography are typically analyzed under the assumption that devices emit independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) states or channels. In realistic experiments, however, noise, drift, feedback, or adversarial behavior violate this assumption. We show that projected least-squares tomography remains statistically optimal even under fully adaptive state and channel preparation. Specifically, we prove that the sample complexity for reconstructing the time-averaged state or channel matches the optimal i.i.d. scaling for non-adaptive, single-copy measurements. For rank-$r$ states, the sample complexity is $\\mathcal{O}(d r^2/ε^2)$ to achieve accuracy $ε$ in trace distance, while for process tomography it is $\\mathcal{O}(d^6/ε^2)$ to achieve accuracy $ε$ in diamond distance. Thus, dropping the i.i.d. assumption does not increase the fundamental sample complexity of quantum tomography, but only changes the interpretation of the reconstructed object."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22057","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:12:46Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:12:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-16T02:36:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21247","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21247","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21247","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Rubel","name":"Rubel, Tobias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laxman","familyName":"Dhulipala","name":"Dhulipala, Laxman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars","familyName":"Gottesbüren","name":"Gottesbüren, Lars","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajesh","familyName":"Jayaram","name":"Jayaram, Rajesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Łącki","name":"Łącki, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PiPNN: Ultra-Scalable Graph-Based Nearest Neighbor Indexing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-17T02:18:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-24T00:23:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T00:49:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-10T19:02:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-14T00:05:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The fastest indexes for Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search today are also the slowest to build: graph-based methods like HNSW and Vamana achieve state-of-the-art query performance but have large construction times due to relying on random-access-heavy beam searches. We introduce PiPNN (Pick-in-Partitions Nearest Neighbors), an ultra-scalable graph construction algorithm that avoids this ``search bottleneck'' that existing graph-based methods suffer from.\n PiPNN's core innovation is HashPrune, a novel online pruning algorithm which dynamically maintains sparse collections of edges. HashPrune enables PiPNN to partition the dataset into overlapping sub-problems, efficiently perform bulk distance comparisons via dense matrix multiplication kernels, and stream a subset of the edges into HashPrune. HashPrune guarantees bounded memory during index construction which permits PiPNN to build higher quality indices without the use of extra intermediate memory.\n PiPNN builds state-of-the-art indexes up to 11.6x faster than Vamana (DiskANN) and up to 12.9x faster than HNSW. PiPNN is significantly more scalable than recent algorithms for fast graph construction. PiPNN builds indexes at least 19.1x faster than MIRAGE and 17.3x than FastKCNA while producing indexes that achieve higher query throughput. PiPNN enables us to build, for the first time, high-quality ANN indexes on billion-scale datasets in under 20 minutes using a single multicore machine."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear at KDD'26"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21247","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:33Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-14T04:01:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21315","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21315","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21315","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leslie Ann","familyName":"Goldberg","name":"Goldberg, Leslie Ann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Lapinskas","name":"Lapinskas, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Instability of all Backoff Protocols"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T19:36:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:04:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T18:29:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T02:07:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-06T11:29:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-07T02:14:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper we prove Aldous's conjecture from 1987 that there is no backoff protocol that is stable for any positive arrival rate. The setting is a communication channel for coordinating requests for a shared resource. Each user who wants to access the resource makes a request by sending a message to the channel. The users don't have any way to communicate with each other, except by sending messages to the channel. The operation of the channel proceeds in discrete time steps. If exactly one message is sent to the channel during a time step then this message succeeds (and leaves the system). If multiple messages are sent during a time step then these messages collide. Each of the users that sent these messages therefore waits a random amount of time before re-sending. A backoff protocol is a randomised algorithm for determining how long to wait -- the waiting time is a function of how many collisions a message has had. Specifically, a backoff protocol is described by a send sequence $\\overline{p} = (p_0,p_1,p_2,\\ldots)$. If a message has had $k$ collisions before a time step then, with probability $p_k$, it sends during that time step, whereas with probability $1-p_k$ it is silent (waiting for later). The most famous backoff protocol is binary exponential backoff, where $p_k = 2^{-k}$. Under Kelly's model, in which the number of new messages that arrive in the system at each time step is given by a Poisson random variable with mean $λ$, Aldous proved that binary exponential backoff is unstable for any positive $λ$. He conjectured that the same is true for any backoff protocol. We prove this conjecture."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21315","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:14Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T05:03:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21263","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21263","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21263","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Nayeri","name":"Nayeri, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inflationary branch decoherence and the cosmological arrow of time"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T05:02:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T02:02:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-18T01:31:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T00:34:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-10T19:18:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-01T01:06:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/1k1w-c7vd","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We analyze branch decoherence in inflationary quantum cosmology by computing reduced density matrices and branch-overlap factors for long-wavelength perturbations. The Hartle-Hawking no-boundary state is real in the semiclassical regime and contains both expanding and contracting WKB components, whereas the tunneling state is selected as an outgoing complex WKB branch; expanding-contracting decoherence is therefore central for the former and mainly diagnostic for the latter. Using the influence-functional formalism, we derive the noise kernel for a light spectator environment and evaluate decoherence under horizon-based and EFT-motivated coarse grainings. We then compute the single-mode branch overlap directly from the Bunch-Davies mode functions, obtaining $|\\mathcal{D}_k(z)|=[z^2/(z^2+1)]^{1/4}$ in the massless limit and $|\\mathcal{D}_k(z)|\\sim z^ν$ on superhorizon scales for massive fields, where $z=-kη$ is the dimensionless wavenumber with $η$ the conformal time. In the massless case, the accumulated geometric branch functional is evaluated in closed form, with a leading cutoff-sensitive phase-space term and a universal subleading contribution. The calculation provides an explicit quantitative bridge between quantum-cosmological boundary conditions, inflationary squeezing, and the emergence of effectively classical cosmological histories."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in Physical Review D; 12 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21263","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-01T02:29:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22128","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22128","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22128","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Chatagnon","name":"Chatagnon, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V.","familyName":"Kubarovsky","name":"Kubarovsky, V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R.","familyName":"Paremuzyan","name":"Paremuzyan, R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Stepanyan","name":"Stepanyan, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Tenorio","name":"Tenorio, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R.","familyName":"Tyson","name":"Tyson, R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. G.","familyName":"Acar","name":"Acar, A. G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Achenbach","name":"Achenbach, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. S.","familyName":"Alvarado","name":"Alvarado, J. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. J.","familyName":"Amaryan","name":"Amaryan, M. J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"W. R.","familyName":"Armstrong","name":"Armstrong, W. R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Avakian","name":"Avakian, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdenti