milyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. P.","familyName":"Watts","name":"Watts, D. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"U.","familyName":"Weerasinghe","name":"Weerasinghe, U.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"X.","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. H.","familyName":"Wood","name":"Wood, M. H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Z.","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Z. W.","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Z. W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V.","familyName":"Ziegler","name":"Ziegler, V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Zurek","name":"Zurek, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Measurement of the near-threshold J$/ψ$ photoproduction cross section with the CLAS12 experiment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Experiment (nucl-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:17:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:05:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-11T11:10:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T00:43:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/lqv1-kf2n","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present measurements of the total and differential cross sections for near-threshold J/$ψ$ photoproduction obtained with the CLAS12 detector at the Thomas Jefferson National Accelerator Facility. The results are based on data collected during the Fall 2018 and Spring 2019 running periods, using electron beams with energies of 10.6 and 10.2 GeV, respectively, scattered off a liquid-hydrogen target. Near-threshold J$/ψ$ photoproduction offers a unique sensitivity to the strong interaction in the non-perturbative regime of Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD). The energy dependence of the cross section constrains the underlying J$/ψ$ production mechanisms, including multi-gluon exchange and potential baryonic excitations. Additionally, the $t$-dependence of the differential cross section can be related to the transverse spatial distribution of gluons in the proton, providing critical input for theoretical descriptions of the gluonic structure of the proton. An interpretation of the results in terms of the gluon content of the proton is presented, providing new experimental constraints on QCD-inspired models of the proton structure and the role of gluonic degrees of freedom in hadronic mass generation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22128","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:32Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-12T02:22:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21949","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21949","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21949","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yahao","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinchao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yinchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Zhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Shikh-Bahaei","name":"Shikh-Bahaei, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Energy Efficient Federated Learning with Hyperdimensional Computing over Wireless Communication Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:33:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:54:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-08T16:45:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-09T02:02:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we investigate a problem of minimizing total energy consumption for secure federated learning (FL) over wireless edge networks. To address the high computational cost and privacy challenges in conventional FL with neural networks (NN) for resource-constrained users, we propose a novel FL with hyperdimensional computing and differential privacy (FL-HDC-DP) framework. In the considered model, each edge user employs hyperdimensional computing (HDC) for local training, which replaces complex neural updates with simple hypervector operations, and applies differential privacy (DP) noise to protect transmitted model information. We optimize the total energy of computation and communication under both latency and privacy constraints. We formulate the problem as an optimization that minimizes the total energy of all users by jointly allocating HDC dimension, transmission time, system bandwidth, transmit power, and CPU frequency. To solve this problem, a sigmoid-variant function is proposed to characterize the relationship between the HDC dimension and the convergence rounds required to reach a target accuracy. Based on this model, we develop two alternating optimization algorithms, where closed-form expressions for time, frequency, bandwidth, and power allocations are derived at each iteration. Since the iterative algorithm requires a feasible initialization, we construct a feasibility problem and obtain feasible initial resource parameters by solving a per round transmission time minimization problem. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed FL-HDC-DP framework achieves up to 83.3% total energy reduction compared with the baseline, while attaining about 90% accuracy in approximately 3.5X fewer communication rounds than the NN baseline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21949","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:07Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:19:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21788","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21788","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21788","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Scaling of LLM Training with Flexible Context Parallelism"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:11:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:44:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-08T12:37:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:44:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scaling long-context capabilities is crucial for Large Language Models (LLMs). However, real-world data contain a large number of sequences with heterogeneous lengths. Existing training libraries for LLMs rely on static parallelism strategies, which suffer from severe load imbalance, redundant communication, and suboptimal hardware utilization under data heterogeneity. In this work, we propose Flexible Context Parallelism (FCP), an efficient parallelism strategy that adaptively reconfigures communication groups and context parallelism degrees during LLM training. We generalize more flexible non-power-of-two parallelism degrees and develop a polynomial-time algorithm to generate near-optimal parallelism strategies with only millisecond-level overhead per training batch. FCP is able to maintain high hardware efficiency even under extreme data heterogeneity. Experimental results demonstrate that FCP significantly outperforms Megatron-LM and DeepSpeed in both LLM and MLLM training, achieving up to 1.46x speedup in average throughput while maintaining near-linear scaling efficiency across large-scale clusters. For extremely unbalanced batches, FCP even achieves 2.24x speedup."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21788","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:19:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21411","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21411","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21411","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Dufay","name":"Dufay, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diana","familyName":"Ghinea","name":"Ghinea, Diana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton","familyName":"Paramonov","name":"Paramonov, Anton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"General Convex Agreement with Near-Optimal Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:31:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:10:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-05T14:52:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T00:50:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Byzantine Agreement (BA) considers a setting of $n$ parties out of which up to $t$ can be byzantine (malicious), and requires the honest parties to agree on an input subject to a condition called \\emph{validity}: if all honest parties have input $v$, the output agreed upon must be $v$. Convex Agreement (CA) strengthens BA by requiring the output agreed upon to lie in the convex hull of the honest parties' inputs. This validity condition captures aggregation tasks, such as robust learning and sensor fusion, where honest inputs may differ but should still constrain the final decision. Existing protocols for CA over general convexity spaces require at least $O(L \\cdot n^2)$ bits of communication for $L$-bit inputs, leaving a gap with BA's $Ω(L \\cdot n)$ lower bound.\n We investigate this gap, and we present deterministic synchronous CA protocols with near-optimal communication complexity in the long-message regime. When $L=Ω(n\\cdotκ)$, where $κ$ is a security parameter, our protocols use $\\mathcal{O}(L\\cdot n\\log n)$ bits of communication for finite convexity spaces and $\\mathcal{O}(L\\cdot n^{1+o(1)})$ communication for Euclidean spaces $\\mathbb{R}^d$. Our protocols also have asymptotically optimal round complexity $\\mathcal{O}(n)$. If an upper bound $L$ on the honest inputs' length in bits is known in advance, we achieve near-optimal resilience $t0$, where $ω$ is the Helly number of the convexity space. When no such bound is known, we achieve resilience $t"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Working paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21411","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:36Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-08T02:04:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sinan","familyName":"Bugu","name":"Bugu, Sinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Single-Shot Fidelity: Chernoff-Based Throughput Optimization in Superconducting Qubit Readout"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:21:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:08:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T18:36:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T02:02:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T14:55:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-05T02:03:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-10T17:15:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-11T01:13:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-02T15:01:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-03T01:05:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Single-shot fidelity is the standard benchmark for superconducting qubit readout, yet it does not directly minimize the wall-clock time needed to certify a quantum state. We treat the dispersive measurement record as a stochastic communication channel and compute the classical Chernoff information governing the multi-shot error exponent, using a trajectory model that incorporates T1 relaxation with full cavity memory. The integration time that maximizes single-shot fidelity and the time that minimizes total certification time do not coincide. For representative transmon parameters and hardware overheads, the throughput-optimal window is longer, cutting certification time by roughly 9-11%, with the gain saturating near 1.13x in the high-readout-power and high-overhead regime. Benchmarking the extracted classical information against the unit-efficiency Gaussian Chernoff limit defines an information-extraction efficiency: dispersive schemes capture ~45% at short integration times, dropping to eta_info(tau_rate) ~ 12% at tau_rate ~ 1.22 us as T1-induced trajectory smearing accumulates. These results connect readout calibration directly to the operational objective of minimizing certification time in high-throughput superconducting processors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:42Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-03T03:14:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21890","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21890","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21890","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baptiste","familyName":"Jego","name":"Jego, Baptiste","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthieu","familyName":"Béthermin","name":"Béthermin, Matthieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katarina","familyName":"Kraljic","name":"Kraljic, Katarina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clotilde","familyName":"Laigle","name":"Laigle, Clotilde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"La Marca","name":"La Marca, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Ilbert","name":"Ilbert, Olivier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hollis B.","familyName":"Akins","name":"Akins, Hollis B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caitlin M.","familyName":"Casey","name":"Casey, Caitlin M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gavin","familyName":"Leroy","name":"Leroy, Gavin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Hadi","name":"Hadi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeyhan S.","familyName":"Kartaltepe","name":"Kartaltepe, Jeyhan S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton M.","familyName":"Koekemoer","name":"Koekemoer, Anton M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry Joy","familyName":"McCracken","name":"McCracken, Henry Joy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louise","familyName":"Paquereau","name":"Paquereau, Louise","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Rhodes","name":"Rhodes, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brant E.","familyName":"Robertson","name":"Robertson, Brant E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marko","familyName":"Shuntov","name":"Shuntov, Marko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Greta","familyName":"Toni","name":"Toni, Greta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The impact of cosmic filaments on starburst galaxies across cosmic times"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:13:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:50:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-02T14:02:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-03T01:01:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cosmological simulations suggest that various galaxy properties depend on their location within the cosmic web. Yet direct observational evidence of the dependence of star formation activity on distance to filaments remains scarce and is missing at z\u0026gt;1. We investigate how starburst, main-sequence (MS), and quenched galaxies are distributed with respect to cosmic web filaments, and how this distribution evolves with redshift. We first use the SIMBA cosmological simulation to predict the redshift evolution of the mean distance to the closest filament from z=3 to z=0 for different galaxy populations after removing stellar-mass dependencies. We then measure the corresponding signal in the COSMOS field, using COSMOS2020 and COSMOS-Web data, where accurate photometric redshifts enable reconstruction of the projected cosmic web from z=2 to z=0.5, and starbursts are identified through far-infrared spectral energy distribution fitting. In agreement with the results from SIMBA, starburst galaxies are found closer to filaments at z\u0026gt;1 and at larger distances at z\u0026lt;1, MS galaxies occupy intermediate environments with little evolution, and quenched galaxies show progressively shorter distances to filaments toward low redshift, with a crossing between starburst and MS populations around z~1. In COSMOS-Web, the relative evolution in the average distance to filaments between starburst and MS galaxies is detected at a significance level of at least 5σ. We show that a minimal toy model in which the only environmental ingredient is the sSFR-filament distance modulation measured in simulations is sufficient to reproduce the observed differential evolution of the average filament distance between starburst and MS galaxies. These results show that the imprint of large-scale environmental effects on the star formation activity of galaxies, predicted by simulations, is detectable from z=2 down to z=0.5."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in A\u0026amp;A"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21890","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-03T03:14:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22101","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22101","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22101","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pantia-Marina","familyName":"Alchirch","name":"Alchirch, Pantia-Marina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitrios I.","familyName":"Diochnos","name":"Diochnos, Dimitrios I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Imbalanced Regression with Hoeffding Trees"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:48:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:03:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-04T23:28:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-06T01:13:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-01T02:18:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:30:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many real-world applications generate continuous data streams for regression. Hoeffding trees and their variants have a long-standing tradition due to their effectiveness, either alone or as base models in broader ensembles. Recent batch-learning work shows that kernel density estimation (KDE) improves smoothed predictions in imbalanced regression [Yang et al., 2021], while hierarchical shrinkage (HS) provides post-hoc regularization for decision trees without modifying their structure [Agarwal et al., 2022]. We extend KDE to streaming settings via a telescoping formulation and integrate HS into incremental decision trees. Empirical evaluation on standard online regression benchmarks shows that KDE consistently improves early-stream performance, whereas HS provides limited gains. Our implementation is publicly available at: https://github.com/marinaAlchirch/DSFA_2026."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 5 figures, 3 tables, 2 algorithms, authors' version of paper accepted in PAKDD 2026 special session on Data Science: Foundations and Applications (DSFA)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22101","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:52Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T05:01:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22045","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22045","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22045","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Walter Hernandez","familyName":"Cruz","name":"Cruz, Walter Hernandez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Devine","name":"Devine, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikhil","familyName":"Vadgama","name":"Vadgama, Nikhil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paolo","familyName":"Tasca","name":"Tasca, Paolo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jiahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DLT-Corpus: A Large-Scale Text Collection for the Distributed Ledger Technology Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:53:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:00:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-27T22:55:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T00:16:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3770855.3817553","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce DLT-Corpus, the largest domain-specific text collection for Distributed Ledger Technology (DLT) research to date: 2.98 billion tokens from 22.12 million documents spanning scientific literature (37,440 publications), United States Patent and Trademark Office (USPTO) patents (49,023 filings), and social media (22 million posts). Existing Natural Language Processing (NLP) resources for DLT focus narrowly on cryptocurrency price prediction and smart contracts, leaving domain-specific language underexplored despite the sector's ~$3 trillion market capitalization and rapid technological evolution.\n We demonstrate DLT-Corpus' utility by analyzing patterns of technology emergence and market-innovation correlations. Findings reveal that technologies first appear in our scientific literature subset before reaching patents and social media, following traditional technology transfer patterns. While social media sentiment remains overwhelmingly bullish even during crypto winters, scientific and patent activity grows less tied to short-term sentiment, tracking overall market expansion in a virtuous cycle in which research precedes and enables economic growth that, in turn, funds further innovation.\n We release the DLT-Corpus and companion artifacts: LedgerBERT (+23% over BERT-base on DLT-specific Named Entity Recognition (NER) task), a sentiment analysis dataset of 23,301 crypto news headlines and descriptions, tools, and code."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Proceedings of the 32nd ACM SIGKDD Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining V.2 (KDD '26)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22045","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:12:29Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:12:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-29T03:01:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21451","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21451","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21451","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroshi","familyName":"Yamaguchi","name":"Yamaguchi, Hiroshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Motoki","familyName":"Asano","name":"Asano, Motoki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Topological phase dynamics described by overtone-synthesized classical and quantum Adler equations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:06:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-21T00:19:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/vcqz-m2r1","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Adler equation is a well-known one-dimensional model describing phase locking and synchronization. Motivated by recent experiments using optomechanical oscillators, we extend the model to include overtone-synthesized sinusoidal coupling with adiabatic temporal modulation. This extension gives rise to unique topological features such as winding-number quantization, discontinuous phase-slip transitions, and hysteretic and non-reciprocal phase dynamics. We further extend the analysis to the quantum regime, where we find a counterintuitive result: the breakdown of winding-number quantization. This arises from the superposition of different winding-number states in a closed-space Thouless pump. Moreover, hysteretic dynamics, once eliminated in quantum adiabatic approximation, is recovered in non-adiabatic calculations, as the superposition of two Floquet states with different PT eigenvalues becomes the quantum counterpart of phase trajectory."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Main text: 11 pages, 5 figures; Supplemental Materials included in the PDF (total: 25 pages, 6 figures)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21451","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:33Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-21T02:39:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22029","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22029","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22029","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang-Duo","familyName":"Tsai","name":"Tsai, Fang-Duo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-An","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Yi-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei-Yueh","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Fei-Yueh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsueh-Wei","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Hsueh-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Jaw","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wei-Jaw","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao-Chung","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hao-Chung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Hsuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yi-Hsuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MIDI-Informed Singing Accompaniment Generation in a Compositional Song Pipeline"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T06:43:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:59:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-05T13:00:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-06T00:44:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While end-to-end lyrics-to-song models offer convenience for casual users, professional songwriters require score-to-song systems that allow them to retain authorship over the core melody. However, existing score-to-song methods are limited to short-form snippets and fail to maintain coherence in long-form generation, particularly during vocal-silent sections like intros and bridges. To address this long-form bottleneck, we propose MIDI-informed singing accompaniment generation (MIDI-SAG). Unlike conventional audio-only models, MIDI-SAG utilizes symbolic timing and chord information derived from the vocal MIDI to provide a stable musical roadmap. By incorporating structure planning, which defines temporal boundaries and semantic labels, our framework facilitates consistent generation across both vocal and non-vocal sections. We demonstrate the feasibility of this compositional pipeline by leveraging specialized pre-trained modules, enabling data-efficient training on a single GPU. Our experiments show the potential of this approach for both professional score-to-song and general lyrics-to-song tasks. While an early exploration, MIDI-SAG suggests a promising direction for structured, long-form music synthesis. Audio demos are available, and the code will be open-sourced at https://composerflow.github.io/web_revealed/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22029","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:12:06Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:12:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-06T02:18:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21801","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21801","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21801","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mauro","familyName":"Marchese","name":"Marchese, Mauro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pietro","familyName":"Savazzi","name":"Savazzi, Pietro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Superimposed Cross-Pilots: Addressing Fractional Shifts in DoA-Aided OTFS"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:29:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:45:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-04T10:56:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-05T01:37:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, a novel superimposed pilot scheme, named superimposed cross-pilots, is proposed for fractional parameter estimation in multi-antenna orthogonal time frequency space (OTFS) receivers. Assuming a large uniform linear array (ULA) size at the receiver, the multipath components are separated in the angular domain through a matched filter (MF). It is then shown that the proposed superimposed pilot scheme enables the computation of integrated delay and Doppler profiles by averaging the received delay-Doppler matrix across the Doppler and delay axes, respectively. This procedure helps reduce data-to-pilot interference via data averaging, eliminating the need for iterative cancellation schemes. Based on this, a fractional parameter estimation algorithm, which exploits MFs, is derived. Simulation results show that the proposed approach outperforms existing OTFS superimposed pilot schemes, achieving a lower bit error rate (BER) while exhibiting a trade-off between peak-to-average power ratio (PAPR) and communication performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21801","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:30Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T04:07:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22187","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22187","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22187","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vipin Singh","familyName":"Sehrawat","name":"Sehrawat, Vipin Singh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UC-Secure Star DKG for Non-Exportable Key Shares with VSS-Free Enforcement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:32:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:09:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-05T18:57:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-06T02:14:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T16:10:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-22T01:09:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Distributed Key Generation (DKG) lets parties derive a common public key while keeping the signing key secret-shared. UC-secure DKG requires a verifiable-sharing enforcement layer -- classically satisfied via Verifiable Secret Sharing (VSS) and/or commitment-and-proof mechanisms -- for secrecy, uniqueness, and affine consistency. We target the Non-eXportable Key (NXK) setting enforced by hardware-backed key-isolation modules (e.g., TEEs, HSM-like APIs), formalized via an ideal KeyBox (keystore) functionality $\\mathcal{F}_{KeyBox}$ that keeps shares non-exportable and permits only attested KeyBox-to-KeyBox sealing. With confidentiality delegated to the NXK boundary, the remaining challenge is enforcing transcript-defined affine consistency without exporting or resharing shares. State continuity rules out rewinding-based extraction, mandating straight-line techniques.\n We combine (i) KeyBox confidentiality; (ii) Unique Structure Verification (USV), a publicly verifiable certificate whose certified scalar never leaves the KeyBox yet whose public group element is transcript-derivable; and (iii) Fischlin-based UC-extractable NIZK arguments of knowledge in a gRO-CRP (global Random Oracle with Context-Restricted Programmability) model. We construct Star DKG (SDKG), a UC-secure scheme for multi-device threshold wallets where a designated service must co-sign but cannot sign alone, realizing a 1+1-out-of-$n$ star access structure (center plus any leaf) over roles (primary vs. recovery) with role-based device registration. In the $\\mathcal{F}_{KeyBox}$-hybrid and gRO-CRP models, under DL and DDH assumptions with adaptive corruptions and secure erasures, SDKG UC-realizes a transcript-driven refinement of the standard UC-DKG functionality. Over a prime-order group of size $p$, SDKG incurs $\\widetilde{O}(n\\log p)$ communication overhead and $\\widetilde{O}(n\\log^{2.585}p)$ bit-operation cost."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22187","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:01Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:34:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22095","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22095","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22095","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Doukas","name":"Doukas, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the emergence of quantum mechanics from stochastic processes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:43:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:03:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-26T14:59:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-27T01:00:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The stochastic--quantum correspondence reinterprets quantum dynamics as arising from an underlying stochastic process on a configuration space. We generalize the correspondence by lifting an arbitrary stochastic kernel $Γ$ in finite dimension to a map $ϕ$ on $B(\\mathcal H)$, formulating the associated lift-compatibility relation, and giving an explicit dictionary between $Γ$ and CPTP (Kraus) maps. We isolate Chapman--Kolmogorov divisibility of the lifted family as the decisive additional constraint: when a CK-consistent CPTP family exists, the lift admits a Lindblad master equation form. In this picture, off-diagonal (phase) degrees of freedom act as a compressed carrier of history dependence not fixed by transition kernels alone; conversely, the apparent emergence of quantum phase information from a phase-blind stochastic description is explained as a memory effect. Finally, we state and prove a divisibility criterion for the underlying stochastic kernels, expressed as a condition involving divisibility of the lifted map together with a diagonality requirement on the density operator."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 1 figure. v2: corrected typos and expanded the discussion in Section 6"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22095","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:43Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:25:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21591","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21591","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21591","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xihua","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Xihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CADC: Content Adaptive Diffusion-Based Generative Image Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:35:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:27:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T16:37:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T03:21:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-25T14:15:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:57:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion-based generative image compression has demonstrated remarkable potential for achieving realistic reconstruction at ultra-low bitrates. The key to unlocking this potential lies in making the entire compression process content-adaptive, ensuring that the encoder's representation and the decoder's generative prior are dynamically aligned with the semantic and structural characteristics of the input image. However, existing methods suffer from three critical limitations that prevent effective content adaptation. First, isotropic quantization applies a uniform quantization step, failing to adapt to the spatially varying complexity of image content and creating a misalignment with the diffusion model's noise-dependent prior. Second, the information concentration bottleneck -- arising from the dimensional mismatch between the high-dimensional noisy latent and the diffusion decoder's fixed input -- prevents the model from adaptively preserving essential semantic information in the primary channels. Third, existing textual conditioning strategies either need significant textual bitrate overhead or rely on generic, content-agnostic textual prompts, thereby failing to provide adaptive semantic guidance efficiently. To overcome these limitations, we propose a content-adaptive diffusion-based image codec with three technical innovations: 1) an Uncertainty-Guided Adaptive Quantization method that learns spatial uncertainty maps to adaptively align quantization distortion with content characteristics; 2) an Auxiliary Decoder-Guided Information Concentration method that uses a lightweight auxiliary decoder to enforce content-aware information preservation in the primary latent channels; and 3) a Bitrate-Free Adaptive Textual Conditioning method that derives content-aware textual descriptions from the auxiliary reconstructed image, enabling semantic guidance without bitrate cost."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21591","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:04Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:34:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21233","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21233","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21233","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Cen","name":"Cen, Rui","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"QiangQiang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, QiangQiang","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Hong","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hong","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Song","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Song","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Lin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yifan","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Decheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Decheng","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Linchuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Linchuan","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Rubing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Rubing","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Guanghua","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Guanghua","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Hunyuan AI Infra Team"],"givenName":"Jianchen","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jianchen","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AngelSlim: A more accessible, comprehensive, and efficient toolkit for large model compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-07T07:02:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T04:50:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:24:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-23T12:35:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:53:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This technical report introduces AngelSlim, a comprehensive and versatile toolkit for large model compression developed by the Tencent Hunyuan team. By consolidating cutting-edge algorithms, including quantization, speculative decoding, token pruning, and distillation. AngelSlim provides a unified pipeline that streamlines the transition from model compression to industrial-scale deployment. To facilitate efficient acceleration, we integrate state-of-the-art FP8 and INT8 Post-Training Quantization (PTQ) algorithms alongside pioneering research in ultra-low-bit regimes, featuring HY-1.8B-int2 as the first industrially viable 2-bit large model. Beyond quantization, we propose a training-aligned speculative decoding framework compatible with multimodal architectures and modern inference engines, achieving 1.8x to 2.0x throughput gains without compromising output correctness. Furthermore, we develop a training-free sparse attention framework that reduces Time-to-First-Token (TTFT) in long-context scenarios by decoupling sparse kernels from model architectures through a hybrid of static patterns and dynamic token selection. For multimodal models, AngelSlim incorporates specialized pruning strategies, namely IDPruner for optimizing vision tokens via Maximal Marginal Relevance and Samp for adaptive audio token merging and pruning. By integrating these compression strategies from low-level implementations, AngelSlim enables algorithm-focused research and tool-assisted deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21233","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:24:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21566","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21566","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21566","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhao","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Yunhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harunari","familyName":"Takata","name":"Takata, Harunari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michail","familyName":"Bachras","name":"Bachras, Michail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqiu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiquan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shiquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gengrui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Gengrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hans-Arno","familyName":"Jacobsen","name":"Jacobsen, Hans-Arno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Epoch-based Optimistic Concurrency Control in Geo-replicated Databases"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:44:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:25:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-16T02:58:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-17T01:46:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3802052","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Geo-distribution is essential for modern online applications to ensure service reliability and high availability. However, supporting high-performance serializable transactions in geo-replicated databases remains a significant challenge. This difficulty stems from the extensive over-coordination inherent in distributed atomic commitment, concurrency control, and fault-tolerance replication protocols under high network latency.\n To address these challenges, we introduce Minerva, a unified distributed concurrency control designed for highly scalable multi-leader replication. Minerva employs a novel epoch-based asynchronous replication protocol that decouples data propagation from the commitment process, enabling continuous transaction replication. Optimistic concurrency control is used to allow any replicas to execute transactions concurrently and commit without coordination. In stead of aborting transactions when conflicts are detected, Minerva uses deterministic re-execution to resolve conflicts, ensuring serializability without sacrificing performance. To further enhance concurrency, we construct a conflict graph and use a maximum weight independent set algorithm to select the optimal subset of transactions for commitment, minimizing the number of re-executed transactions. Our evaluation demonstrates that Minerva significantly outperforms state-of-the-art replicated databases, achieving over $3\\times$ higher throughput in scalability experiments and $2.8\\times$ higher throughput during a high network latency simulation with the TPC-C benchmark."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21566","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:25Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-17T05:05:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21667","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21667","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21667","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enshu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Enshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfei","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Hongfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Teng","name":"Teng, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libing","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Libing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunming","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Chunming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Send Less, Perceive More: Masked Quantized Point Cloud Communication for Loss-Tolerant Collaborative Perception"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:00:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:33:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-15T11:42:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-17T01:16:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Collaborative perception allows connected vehicles to overcome occlusions and limited viewpoints by sharing sensory information. However, existing approaches struggle to achieve high accuracy under strict bandwidth constraints and remain highly vulnerable to random transmission packet loss. We introduce QPoint2Comm, a quantized point-cloud communication framework that dramatically reduces bandwidth while preserving high-fidelity 3D information. Instead of transmitting intermediate features, QPoint2Comm directly communicates quantized point-cloud indices using a shared codebook, enabling efficient reconstruction with lower bandwidth than feature-based methods. To ensure robustness to possible communication packet loss, we employ a masked training strategy that simulates random packet loss, allowing the model to maintain strong performance even under severe transmission failures. In addition, a cascade attention fusion module is proposed to enhance multi-vehicle information integration. Extensive experiments on both simulated and real-world datasets demonstrate that QPoint2Comm sets a new state of the art in accuracy, communication efficiency, and resilience to packet loss."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21667","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:08Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-17T05:05:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21273","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21273","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21273","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"GuoHua","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, GuoHua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StoryTailor:A Zero-Shot Pipeline for Action-Rich Multi-Subject Visual Narratives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:07:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-06T08:35:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:32:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating multi-frame, action-rich visual narratives without fine-tuning faces a threefold tension: action text faithfulness, subject identity fidelity, and cross-frame background continuity. We propose StoryTailor, a zero-shot pipeline that runs on a single RTX 4090 (24 GB) and produces temporally coherent, identity-preserving image sequences from a long narrative prompt, per-subject references, and grounding boxes. Three synergistic modules drive the system: Gaussian-Centered Attention (GCA) to dynamically focus on each subject core and ease grounding-box overlaps; Action-Boost Singular Value Reweighting (AB-SVR) to amplify action-related directions in the text embedding space; and Selective Forgetting Cache (SFC) that retains transferable background cues, forgets nonessential history, and selectively surfaces retained cues to build cross-scene semantic ties. Compared with baseline methods, experiments show that CLIP-T improves by up to 10-15%, with DreamSim lower than strong baselines, while CLIP-I stays in a visually acceptable, competitive range. With matched resolution and steps on a 24 GB GPU, inference is faster than FluxKontext. Qualitatively, StoryTailor delivers expressive interactions and evolving yet stable scenes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages,19 figures,accepted by CVPR2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21273","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:13:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22134","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22134","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22134","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengsi","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Chengsi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeming","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Zeming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyong","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiaoyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dusit","familyName":"Niyato","name":"Niyato, Dusit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Secure Semantic Communications via AI Defenses: Fundamentals, Solutions, and Future Directions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:28:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:06:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-04T08:33:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-05T01:40:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semantic communication (SemCom) redefines wireless communication from reproducing symbols to transmitting task-relevant semantics. However, this AI-native architecture also introduces new vulnerabilities, as semantic failures may arise from adversarial perturbations to models, corrupted training data, desynchronized priors, or misaligned inference even when lower-layer transmission reliability and cryptographic protection remain intact. This survey provides a defense-centered and system-oriented synthesis of security in SemCom via AI defense. We analyze AI-centric threat models by consolidating existing studies and organizing attack surfaces across model-level, channel-realizable, knowledge-based, and networked inference vectors. Building on this foundation, we present a structured taxonomy of defense strategies organized by where semantic integrity can be compromised in SemCom systems despite correct symbol delivery, spanning semantic encoding, wireless transmission, knowledge integrity, and coordination among multiple agents. These categories correspond to distinct security failure modes, including representation fragility, channel-realizable manipulation, semantic prior poisoning or desynchronization, and adversarial propagation through distributed inference. We also examine security utility operating envelopes that capture tradeoffs among semantic fidelity, robustness, latency, and energy under realistic constraints, survey evaluation frameworks and representative applications, and identify open challenges in cross-layer composition and deployment-time certification. Overall, this survey offers a unified system-level perspective that enables readers to understand major threat and defense mechanisms in AI-native SemCom systems and to leverage emerging security techniques in the design and deployment of robust SemCom architectures for next-generation intelligent networks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22134","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-05T03:49:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22136","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22136","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22136","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qunyou","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qunyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengbo","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Pengbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marina","familyName":"Zapater","name":"Zapater, Marina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Atienza","name":"Atienza, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SigmaQuant: Hardware-Aware Heterogeneous Quantization Method for Edge DNN Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:34:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:06:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T14:17:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:55:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep neural networks (DNNs) are essential for performing advanced tasks on edge or mobile devices, yet their deployment is often hindered by severe resource constraints, including limited memory, energy, and computational power. While uniform quantization provides a straightforward approach to compress model and reduce hardware requirement, it fails to fully leverage the varying robustness across layers, and often lead to accuracy degradation or suboptimal resource usage, particularly at low bitwidths. In contrast, heterogeneous quantization, which allocates different bitwidths to individual layers, can mitigate these drawbacks. Nonetheless, current heterogeneous quantization methods either needs huge brute-force design space search or lacks the adaptability to meet different hardware conditions, such as memory size, energy budget, and latency requirement. Filling these gaps, this work introduces \\textbf{\\textit{SigmaQuant}}, an adaptive layer-wise heterogeneous quantization framework designed to efficiently balance accuracy and resource usage for varied edge environments without exhaustive search."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22136","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:44Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:23:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21626","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21626","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21626","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gholamreza","familyName":"Haffari","name":"Haffari, Gholamreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minxian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Minxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajkumar","familyName":"Buyya","name":"Buyya, Rajkumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adel N.","familyName":"Toosi","name":"Toosi, Adel N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Layer Scheduling for MoE-Based LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:42:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T23:30:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:13:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success across a wide range of tasks, but serving them efficiently at scale remains a critical challenge due to their substantial computational and latency demands. While most existing inference frameworks rely on simple scheduling strategies such as First-Come-First-Serve (FCFS) at the engine level and Round-Robin (RR) at the scheduler or coordinator level, they often fail to fully utilize system resources and may suffer from issues such as head-of-line blocking and load imbalance. Recent advances in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models have also introduced new challenges in scheduling arising from expert parallelism and routing complexity. This research proposes a multi-layer scheduling framework tailored for MoE-based LLM serving. It targets scheduling at three levels: request-level, enginelevel, and expert-level. At the request level, we explore algorithms such as Shortest-Job-First (SJF) and priority-aware aging to improve throughput and reduce latency. At the engine level, we design load-aware dispatching strategies that account for the current prefix token load, KV cache utilization, and user stickiness to achieve better resource matching. At the expert level, we focus on alleviating expert hotspots and strategically placing inter-layer expert dependencies to balance load and improve routing efficiency. Extensive experimental results from more than 100 experiments conducted under diverse workload distributions show that our approach consistently outperforms the state-of-theart inference framework vLLM, achieving up to 17.8% reduction in Time To First Token (TTFT) latency and 13.3% reduction in Time-Per-Output-Token (TPOT) latency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21626","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:00Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:23:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22017","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22017","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22017","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Egersdoerfer","name":"Egersdoerfer, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnav","familyName":"Sareen","name":"Sareen, Arnav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean Luca","familyName":"Bez","name":"Bez, Jean Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suren","familyName":"Byna","name":"Byna, Suren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongkuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Dongkuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IOAgent: Democratizing Trustworthy HPC I/O Performance Diagnosis Capability via LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:30:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:58:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T02:25:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:14:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/ipdps64566.2025.00036","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As the complexity of the HPC storage stack rapidly grows, domain scientists face increasing challenges in effectively utilizing HPC storage systems to achieve their desired I/O performance. To identify and address I/O issues, scientists largely rely on I/O experts to analyze their I/O traces and provide insights into potential problems. However, with a limited number of I/O experts and the growing demand for data-intensive applications, inaccessibility has become a major bottleneck, hindering scientists from maximizing their productivity. Rapid advances in LLMs make it possible to build an automated tool that brings trustworthy I/O performance diagnosis to domain scientists. However, key challenges remain, such as the inability to handle long context windows, a lack of accurate domain knowledge about HPC I/O, and the generation of hallucinations during complex interactions. In this work, we propose IOAgent as a systematic effort to address these challenges. IOAgent integrates a module-based pre-processor, a RAG-based domain knowledge integrator, and a tree-based merger to accurately diagnose I/O issues from a given Darshan trace file. Similar to an I/O expert, IOAgent provides detailed justifications and references for its diagnoses and offers an interactive interface for scientists to ask targeted follow-up questions. To evaluate IOAgent, we collected a diverse set of labeled job traces and released the first open diagnosis test suite, TraceBench. Using this test suite, we conducted extensive evaluations, demonstrating that IOAgent matches or outperforms state-of-the-art I/O diagnosis tools with accurate and useful diagnosis results. We also show that IOAgent is not tied to specific LLMs, performing similarly well with both proprietary and open-source LLMs. We believe IOAgent has the potential to become a powerful tool for scientists navigating complex HPC I/O subsystems in the future."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in the Proceedings of the 2025 IEEE International Parallel and Distributed Processing Symposium (IPDPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22017","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:47Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:11:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21897","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21897","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21897","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleix","familyName":"Boné","name":"Boné, Aleix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Aguirre","name":"Aguirre, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Álvarez","name":"Álvarez, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro J.","familyName":"Martinez-Ferrer","name":"Martinez-Ferrer, Pedro J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vicenç","familyName":"Beltran","name":"Beltran, Vicenç","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A task-based data-flow methodology for programming heterogeneous systems with multiple accelerator APIs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:27:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:51:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T09:46:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:36:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.future.2026.108383","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Heterogeneous nodes that combine multi-core CPUs with diverse accelerators are rapidly becoming the norm in both high-performance computing (HPC) and AI infrastructures. Exploiting these platforms, however, requires orchestrating several low-level accelerator APIs such as CUDA, SYCL, and Triton. In some occasions they can be combined with optimized vendor math libraries: e.g., cuBLAS and oneAPI. Each API or library introduces its own abstractions, execution semantics, and synchronization mechanisms. Combining them within a single application is therefore error-prone and labor-intensive. We propose reusing a task-based data-flow methodology together with Task-Aware APIs (TA-libs) to overcome these limitations and facilitate the seamless integration of multiple accelerator programming models, while still leveraging the best-in-class kernels offered by each API.\n Applications are expressed as a directed acyclic graph (DAG) of host tasks and device kernels managed by an OpenMP/OmpSs-2 runtime. We introduce Task-Aware SYCL (TASYCL) and leverage Task-Aware CUDA (TACUDA), which elevate individual accelerator invocations to first-class tasks. When multiple native runtimes coexist on the same multi-core CPU, they contend for threads, leading to oversubscription and performance variability. To address this, we unify their thread management under the nOS-V tasking and threading library, to which we contribute a new port of the PoCL (Portable OpenCL) runtime.\n These results demonstrate that task-aware libraries, coupled with the nOS-V library, enable a single application to harness multiple accelerator programming models transparently and efficiently. The proposed methodology is immediately applicable to current heterogeneous nodes and is readily extensible to future systems that integrate even richer combinations of CPUs, GPUs, FPGAs, and AI accelerators."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21897","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:51Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:11:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21644","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21644","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21644","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-An","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xijia","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Xijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Conghao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Conghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Danyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DAGS-SLAM: Dynamic-Aware 3DGS SLAM via Spatiotemporal Motion Probability and Uncertainty-Aware Scheduling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:14:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:31:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T11:48:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:41:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mobile robots and IoT devices demand real-time localization and dense reconstruction under tight compute and energy budgets. While 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) enables efficient dense SLAM, dynamic objects and occlusions still degrade tracking and mapping. Existing dynamic 3DGS-SLAM often relies on heavy optical flow and per-frame segmentation, which is costly for mobile deployment and brittle under challenging illumination. We present DAGS-SLAM, a dynamic-aware 3DGS-SLAM system that maintains a spatiotemporal motion probability (MP) state per Gaussian and triggers semantics on demand via an uncertainty-aware scheduler. DAGS-SLAM fuses lightweight YOLO instance priors with geometric cues to estimate and temporally update MP, propagates MP to the front-end for dynamic-aware correspondence selection, and suppresses dynamic artifacts in the back-end via MP-guided optimization. Experiments on public dynamic RGB-D benchmarks show improved reconstruction and robust tracking while sustaining real-time throughput on a commodity GPU, demonstrating a practical speed-accuracy tradeoff with reduced semantic invocations toward mobile deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21644","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:30Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:11:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21549","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21549","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21549","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evelyn","familyName":"Voss","name":"Voss, Evelyn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bikun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaoyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Passive Environment-Assisted Quantum Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:14:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:24:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T02:36:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:15:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As quantum information systems mature, efficient and coherent transfer of quantum information through noisy channels becomes increasingly important. We examine how passive environment-assisted quantum communication enhances direct quantum information transfer efficiency. A bosonic pure-loss channel, modeled as transmission through a beam splitter with a vacuum input state at the dark port, has zero quantum capacity when transmissivity is below 50%. Quantum communication through the channel can be enhanced by passive environment assistance, achieved via the selection of an appropriate input state for the ancilla port. Although ideal Gottesman-Kitaev-Preskill (GKP) states enable perfect quantum information transmission at arbitrarily small transmissivity, they are challenging to realize experimentally. We therefore explore more experimentally accessible non-Gaussian ancilla states, such as Fock, cat, and squeezed cat states, and numerically determine the optimal encoding and decoding strategies. We also construct analytical schemes that yield high-fidelity transmission and good information rates."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21549","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:11:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21548","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21548","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21548","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongtong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yongtong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shaoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinmin","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Yinmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rilin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Rilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Liyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangyan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shangyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunfeng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shunfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Mingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panpan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Panpan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DualPath: Breaking the Storage Bandwidth Bottleneck in Agentic LLM Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:10:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:23:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:04:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:15:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The performance of multi-turn, agentic LLM inference is increasingly dominated by KV-Cache storage I/O rather than computation. In prevalent disaggregated architectures, loading the massive KV-Cache from external storage creates a fundamental imbalance: storage NICs on prefill engines become bandwidth-saturated, while those on decoding engines remain idle. This asymmetry severely constrains overall system throughput.\n We present DualPath, an inference system that breaks this bottleneck by introducing dual-path KV-Cache loading. Beyond the traditional storage-to-prefill path, DualPath enables a novel storage-to-decode path, in which the KV-Cache is loaded into decoding engines and then efficiently transferred to prefill engines via RDMA over the compute network. DualPath combines this optimized data path -- which inherently avoids network congestion and avoids interference with latency-critical model execution communications -- with a global scheduler that dynamically balances load across prefill and decode engines.\n Our evaluation on three models with production agentic workloads demonstrates that DualPath improves offline inference throughput by up to 1.87$\\times$ on our in-house inference system. It can also improve online serving throughput by an average factor of 1.96$\\times$ without violating SLO."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21548","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:57Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:20:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22172","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22172","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22172","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhui","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Ziyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianghao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jianghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianyi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Qianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuekai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Letian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Letian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuhong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiuhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixing","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Yixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanying","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yanying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueqin","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xueqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinlu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xinlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Effects of realistic laser intensity and phase distribution on high-charge laser wakefield acceleration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Plasma Physics (physics.plasm-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Accelerator Physics (physics.acc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:18:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:08:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Laser wakefield acceleration (LWFA) can produce relativistic electron beams and various secondary particles in centimeter-long plasmas, making it a valuable particle source with important applications in many disciplines. In this work, we examine the effects of non-ideal transverse intensity and phase distribution of laser pulses on LWFA through both experimental measurements and particle-in-cell simulations. The complex transverse profile of the 75 TW laser pulses reduces the self-focused intensity in plasma compared with a transversely Gaussian laser. Furthermore, the sheath structure of the nonlinear plasma wake excited by realistic laser pulses is wider and more complicated than that of a Gaussian laser. These hinder the injection of the plasma electrons. As the laser pulse propagates through the plasma, its intensity profile gradually becomes elliptical and drives a plasma wake with a sharp sheath near the azimuths of the major axis, leading to an injection. When using a realistic laser profile in simulations, both the charge and energy of injected electrons closely match experimental results ($\\sim200$ pC of charge and $\\sim 200$ MeV peak energy), whereas the Gaussian laser simulations produce much higher charge ($\\sim500$ pC). Our findings reveal the difference in injection dynamics between LWFA driven by non-ideal laser pulses and those driven by Gaussian pulses, and are useful for applications of LWFA which demand high-charge electron beams."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22172","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:40Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:15:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22163","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22163","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22163","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunwoo","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Eunwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BMN-like Matrix Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:13:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:08:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We conjecture a family of matrix quantum mechanical models that are holographically dual to discrete light-cone quantization of M-theory in pp-wave-like backgrounds. These backgrounds can be obtained from a Penrose limit of AdS$_4\\times X_7$, where $X_7$ is Einstein. The matrix models arise from a classically consistent dimensional reduction of the UV Lagrangians of $\\mathcal{N}=1$ superconformal field theories, in close analogy with how the BMN matrix model is obtained by dimensional reduction from $\\mathcal{N}=4$ super Yang-Mills theory. We also discuss about supersymmetric black objects in pp-wave background by studying the Witten index and speculate that the area of the horizon is bounded from above for a fixed $N$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22163","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:26Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:15:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22160","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22160","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22160","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Vokes","name":"Vokes, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinod N.","familyName":"Rao","name":"Rao, Vinod N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elinore","familyName":"Spencer","name":"Spencer, Elinore","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rupesh","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Rupesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Energy efficient optical tracking for space quantum communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:06:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:07:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Power consumption is a critical constraint for CubeSat based quantum communication, where tracking systems often dominate the onboard power budget. We demonstrate an energy-efficient approach that enables reliable satellite tracking at substantially reduced beacon power by treating tracking as a weak-signal estimation task. Using a closed-loop system with fine steering mirrors and higher-order Kalman filters on ground, we can maintain stable tracking at a transmitted power equivalent to 34 mW over a -60 dB satellite to ground optical channel. Our results show that the resulting penalties on QKD bit error rates and signal-to-noise ratios are negligible, allowing for more efficient power allocation to quantum payloads in CubeSat missions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22160","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:22Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:15:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22158","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22158","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22158","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minqiu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Minqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luanzheng","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Luanzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan R.","familyName":"Tallent","name":"Tallent, Nathan R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kento","familyName":"Sato","name":"Sato, Kento","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMTailor: A Layer-wise Tailoring Tool for Efficient Checkpointing of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:05:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:07:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3731599.3767515","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Checkpointing is essential for fault tolerance in training large language models (LLMs). However, existing methods, regardless of their I/O strategies, periodically store the entire model and optimizer states, incurring substantial storage overhead and resource contention. Recent studies reveal that updates across LLM layers are highly non-uniform. Across training steps, some layers may undergo more significant changes, while others remain relatively stable or even unchanged. This suggests that selectively checkpointing only layers with significant updates could reduce overhead without harming training. Implementing such selective strategies requires fine-grained control over both weights and optimizer states, which no current tool provides. To address this gap, we propose \\texttt{LLMTailor}, a checkpoint-merging framework that filters and assembles layers from different checkpoints to form a composite checkpoint. Our evaluation indicates that LLMTailor can work with different selective checkpointing strategies and effectively reduce checkpoint size (e.g., 4.3 times smaller for Llama3.1-8B) and checkpoint time (e.g., 2.8 times faster for Qwen2.5-7B) while maintaining model quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 3 figures, accepted at PDSW'25"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22158","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:19Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:15:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satyam Kumar","familyName":"Navneet","name":"Navneet, Satyam Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joydeep","familyName":"Chandra","name":"Chandra, Joydeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When AI Writes, Whose Voice Remains? Quantifying Cultural Marker Erasure Across World English Varieties in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:54:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:07:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772363.3799085","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used to ``professionalize'' workplace communication, often at the cost of linguistic identity. We introduce \"Cultural Ghosting\", the systematic erasure of linguistic markers unique to non-native English varieties during text processing. Through analysis of 22,350 LLM outputs generated from 1,490 culturally marked texts (Indian, Singaporean,\u0026amp; Nigerian English) processed by five models under three prompt conditions, we quantify this phenomenon using two novel metrics: Identity Erasure Rate (IER) \u0026amp; Semantic Preservation Score (SPS). Across all prompts, we find an overall IER of 10.26%, with model-level variation from 3.5% to 20.5% (5.9x range). Crucially, we identify a Semantic Preservation Paradox: models maintain high semantic similarity (mean SPS = 0.748) while systematically erasing cultural markers. Pragmatic markers (politeness conventions) are 1.9x more vulnerable than lexical markers (71.5% vs. 37.1% erasure). Our experiments demonstrate that explicit cultural-preservation prompts reduce erasure by 29% without sacrificing semantic quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:57Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22131","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22131","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22131","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imran","familyName":"Kabir","name":"Kabir, Imran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon Ann","familyName":"Redmon","name":"Redmon, Sharon Ann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lynn R","familyName":"Elko","name":"Elko, Lynn R","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mitchell A","familyName":"Case","name":"Case, Mitchell A","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawn J","familyName":"Sowers","name":"Sowers, Dawn J","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krista","familyName":"Wilkinson","name":"Wilkinson, Krista","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syed Masum","familyName":"Billah","name":"Billah, Syed Masum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Giving Meaning to Movements: Challenges and Opportunities in Expanding Communication by Pairing Unaided AAC with Speech Generated Messages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:21:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:05:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772318.3791273","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Augmentative and Alternative Communication (AAC) technologies are categorized into two forms: aided AAC, which uses external devices like speech-generating systems to produce standardized output, and unaided AAC, which relies on body-based gestures for natural expression but requires shared understanding. We investigate how to combine these approaches to harness the speed and naturalness of unaided AAC while maintaining the intelligibility of aided AAC, a largely unexplored area for individuals with communication and motor impairments. Through 18 months of participatory design with AAC users, we identified key challenges and opportunities and developed AllyAAC, a wearable system with a wrist-worn IMU paired with a smartphone app. We evaluated AllyAAC in a field study with 14 participants and produced a dataset containing over 600,000 multimodal data points featuring atypical gestures--the first of its kind. Our findings reveal challenges in recognizing personalized, idiosyncratic gestures and demonstrate how to address them using Transformer-based large machine learning (ML) models with different pretraining strategies. In sum, we contribute design principles and a reference implementation for adaptive, personalized systems combining aided and unaided AAC."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in Proceedings of the 2026 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems (CHI '26)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22131","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:37Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22109","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22109","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22109","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Gracar","name":"Gracar, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benedikt","familyName":"Jahnel","name":"Jahnel, Benedikt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Lüchtrath","name":"Lüchtrath, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anh Duc","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Anh Duc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detection, coverage and percolation in dynamic Boolean models with random radii based on $α$-stable processes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60D05, secondary: 60K35","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:57:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:04:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider a dynamic network in continuum time and space in which nodes, with initial locations given by a Poisson point process, move according to i.i.d. isotropic $α$-stable processes. Each node is additionally equipped with an i.i.d. detection radius. Inspired by corresponding results by Peres et. al. on mobile networks based on Brownian sausages with fixed width, we investigate the tail behaviour of three stopping times: The detection time of the first discovery of a designated node, the first coverage of an entire set, and the first discovery of a node by the infinite connected component of the system. Broadly speaking, we discover that the stability index as well as the random radii manifest themselves only in constants in the otherwise exponential decay rates. The proofs rest on heat-kernel bounds for the underlying Lévy processes and a detailed multiscale analysis allowing us to control the space-time correlations of the system."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22109","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:03Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22103","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22103","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22103","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Mao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeran","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Hyeran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keren","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Keren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PASTA: A Modular Program Analysis Tool Framework for Accelerators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:51:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:03:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The increasing complexity and diversity of hardware accelerators in modern computing systems demand flexible, low-overhead program analysis tools. We present PASTA, a low-overhead and modular Program AnalysiS Tool Framework for Accelerators. PASTA abstracts over low-level profiling APIs and diverse deep learning frameworks, offering users a unified interface to capture and analyze runtime events at multiple levels. Its extensible design enables researchers and practitioners to rapidly prototype custom tools with minimal overhead. We demonstrate the utility of PASTA by developing several analysis tools, including a deep learning workload characterization tool and a UVM optimization tool. Through extensive evaluation on mainstream deep learning workloads tested on NVIDIA and AMD GPUs under both single- and multi-GPU scenarios, we demonstrate PASTA's broad applicability. On NVIDIA GPUs, we further show that PASTA provides detailed performance insights with significantly lower overhead, up to 1.3*10^4 faster than conventional analysis tools, thanks to its GPU-accelerated backend. PASTA strikes a practical balance between usability, extensibility, and efficiency, making it well-suited for modern accelerator-based computing environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22103","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:55Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:13:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22102","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22102","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22102","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karolina","familyName":"Dziwulska","name":"Dziwulska, Karolina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Spiess","name":"Spiess, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarika","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Sarika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Leipe","name":"Leipe, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yugant","familyName":"Hadiyal","name":"Hadiyal, Yugant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Steinlechner","name":"Steinlechner, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-stabilized high-dimensional quantum key distribution on a metropolitan free-space link"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:51:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:03:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum communication technologies capable of operating reliably across heterogeneous optical channels are essential for scalable metropolitan quantum networks. Here we demonstrate high-dimensional time-bin-encoded quantum key distribution over a hybrid metropolitan link comprising 1.7 km free-space transmission and 685 m of optical fiber. Operating at a clock rate of 500 MHz in the C-band, we implement both 2- and 4-dimensional protocols, and obtain estimated secure finite-key rates of (95 +- 28) kbit/s for 4D at (25.0 +- 2.0) dB loss and (59 +- 27) kbit/s for 2D at (23.5 +- 2.3) dB loss. Crucially, we achieve continuous operation over 48 h in a fully self-referenced architecture: initial synchronization, interferometric phase stabilization, and long-term drift compensation are performed exclusively using the detected quantum signals, without auxiliary optical reference channels. Our results thus establish a practical and versatile platform for hybrid free-space-to-fiber quantum communication and show that the encoding dimensionality can be adapted to the optimal operating regime of realistic metropolitan channels, providing a pathway toward efficient, autonomous and deployable quantum network nodes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22102","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:53Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:13:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22011","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22011","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22011","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kundan","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Kundan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Generic Web Component for WebRTC Pub-Sub"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:27:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:58:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present video-io, a generic web component to publish or subscribe to a media stream in WebRTC (web real-time communication) applications. Unlike a call or conference room abstraction of existing video conferencing services, it uses a named stream abstraction, which is useful in many scenarios beyond just a call or conference. It keeps most of the application logic in the endpoint using the extensive application interface of this component, and keeps any vendor specific access control or signaling negotiation in a service-specific connector implementation. This allows an app developer to write once, and be able to run the web app on different servers or services. We also demonstrate its flexibility by implementing the connector for ten different existing systems and services. Decoupling the app from the hosted vendor service promotes innovation in the endpoint beyond what a single vendor locked client app can offer."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 12 figures, 6 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22011","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:39Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21998","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21998","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21998","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinran","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xinran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anqi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Anqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Design-based theory for causal inference from adaptive experiments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:17:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:57:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adaptive designs dynamically update treatment probabilities using information accumulated during the experiment. Existing theory for causal inference from adaptive experiments primarily assumes the superpopulation framework with independent and identically distributed units, and may not apply when the distribution of units evolves over time. This paper makes two contributions. First, we extend the literature to the finite-population framework, which allows for possibly nonexchangeable units, and establish the design-based theory for causal inference under general adaptive designs using inverse-propensity-weighted (IPW) and augmented IPW (AIPW) estimators. Our theory accommodates nonexchangeable units, both nonconverging and vanishing treatment probabilities, and nonconverging outcome estimators, thereby justifying inference using AIPW estimators with black-box outcome models that integrate advances from machine learning methods. To alleviate the conservativeness inherent in variance estimation under finite-population inference, we also introduce a covariance estimator for the AIPW estimator that becomes sharp when the residuals from the adaptive regression of potential outcomes on covariates are additive across units. Our framework encompasses widely used adaptive designs, such as multi-armed bandits, covariate-adaptive randomization, and sequential rerandomization, advancing the design-based theory for causal inference in these specific settings. Second, as a methodological contribution, we propose an adaptive covariate adjustment approach for analyzing even nonadaptive designs. The martingale structure induced by adaptive adjustment enables valid inference with black-box outcome estimators that would otherwise require strong assumptions under standard nonadaptive analysis."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21998","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:19Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21997","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21997","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21997","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"WeiZhe","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, WeiZhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanxin","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Fanxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing LLM-Based Test Generation by Eliminating Covered Code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:16:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:57:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated test generation is essential for software quality assurance, with coverage rate serving as a key metric to ensure thorough testing. Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown promise in improving test generation, particularly in achieving higher coverage. However, while existing LLM-based test generation solutions perform well on small, isolated code snippets, they struggle when applied to complex methods under test. To address these issues, we propose a scalable LLM-based unit test generation method. Our approach consists of two key steps. The first step is context information retrieval, which uses both LLMs and static analysis to gather relevant contextual information associated with the complex methods under test. The second step, iterative test generation with code elimination, repeatedly generates unit tests for the code slice, tracks the achieved coverage, and selectively removes code segments that have already been covered. This process simplifies the testing task and mitigates issues arising from token limits or reduced reasoning effectiveness associated with excessively long contexts. Through comprehensive evaluations on open-source projects, our approach outperforms state-of-the-art LLM-based and search-based methods, demonstrating its effectiveness in achieving high coverage on complex methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 4 figures, supplementary material included"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21997","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:18Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21990","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21990","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21990","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. E.","familyName":"Veres","name":"Veres, D. E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Arrutia","name":"Arrutia, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S. Cettour","familyName":"Cave","name":"Cave, S. Cettour","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"B. H. F.","familyName":"Lindström","name":"Lindström, B. H. F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Giovannozzi","name":"Giovannozzi, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L. E.","familyName":"Pauwels","name":"Pauwels, L. E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"F. F.","familyName":"Van der Veken","name":"Van der Veken, F. F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"F. M.","familyName":"Velotti","name":"Velotti, F. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Franchetti","name":"Franchetti, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Proof-of-principle experiment of a novel beam extraction over millions of turns using stable resonance islands and bent crystal"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Accelerator Physics (physics.acc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:10:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:57:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A recent study [1] has introduced an advanced method aimed at extracting from a circular particle accelerator over millions of turns using stable islands and a bent crystal. This technique leverages the strength of non-linear beam dynamics along with adiabatic trapping and transport within stable islands, in combination with a bent silicon crystal, to provide an efficient extraction of hadron beams over millions of turns. The positive and encouraging results of the comprehensive numerical simulations were validated through a successful proof-of-principle experiment at the CERN Super Proton Synchrotron, which demonstrated the feasibility of the technique. This paper presents a detailed discussion and analysis of the experimental results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21990","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:08Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21980","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21980","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21980","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salah","familyName":"Guessoum","name":"Guessoum, Salah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tushar","familyName":"Malica","name":"Malica, Tushar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Athanasios","familyName":"Kyriazis","name":"Kyriazis, Athanasios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jürgen","familyName":"Van Erps","name":"Van Erps, Jürgen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geert","familyName":"Van Steenberge","name":"Van Steenberge, Geert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Virte","name":"Virte, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Polarization Dynamics in VCSELs Under Sinusoidal Signal Modulation around the Polarization Switching point"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:58:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:56:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vertical-Cavity Surface-Emitting Lasers (VCSELs) combine compact geometry, low threshold current, and ease of integration, making them central to modern photonic systems. However, their polarization behavior remains a critical factor affecting performance, as the emission state can switch between orthogonally polarized modes around the so-called polarization switching point. This regime exhibits high sensitivity, where small perturbations induce abrupt polarization changes and nonlinear responses. In this work, the polarization dynamics of VCSELs under sinusoidal current modulation around the switching point are numerically investigated using the Spin-Flip Model. The study examines the influence of modulation frequency, amplitude, and bias current, revealing distinct dynamical regimes including polarization locking, periodic and irregular switching. The observed transitions between regimes elucidate the interplay between modulation and polarization stability, providing insight into the control of VCSEL dynamics for high-speed optical communication and sensing applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21980","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:53Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21957","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21957","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21957","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchun","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Yuchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingli","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Bingli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Collaborate via Structures: Cluster-Guided Item Alignment for Federated Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:39:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:55:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated recommendation facilitates collaborative model training across distributed clients while keeping sensitive user interaction data local. Conventional approaches typically rely on synchronizing high-dimensional item representations between the server and clients. This paradigm implicitly assumes that precise geometric alignment of embedding coordinates is necessary for collaboration across clients. We posit that establishing relative semantic relationships among items is more effective than enforcing shared representations. Specifically, global semantic relations serve as structural constraints for items. Within these constraints, the framework allows item representations to vary locally on each client, which flexibility enables the model to capture fine-grained user personalization while maintaining global consistency. To this end, we propose Cluster-Guided FedRec framework (CGFedRec), a framework that transforms uploaded embeddings into compact cluster labels. In this framework, the server functions as a global structure discoverer to learn item clusters and distributes only the resulting labels. This mechanism explicitly cuts off the downstream transmission of item embeddings, relieving clients from maintaining global shared item embeddings. Consequently, CGFedRec achieves the effective injection of global collaborative signals into local item representations without transmitting full embeddings. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach significantly improves communication efficiency while maintaining superior recommendation accuracy across multiple datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21957","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21928","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21928","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21928","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayush","familyName":"Mohanty","name":"Mohanty, Ayush","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paritosh","familyName":"Ramanan","name":"Ramanan, Paritosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nagi","familyName":"Gebraeel","name":"Gebraeel, Nagi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Unknown Interdependencies for Decentralized Root Cause Analysis in Nonlinear Dynamical Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:05:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:53:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Root cause analysis (RCA) in networked industrial systems, such as supply chains and power networks, is notoriously difficult due to unknown and dynamically evolving interdependencies among geographically distributed clients. These clients represent heterogeneous physical processes and industrial assets equipped with sensors that generate large volumes of nonlinear, high-dimensional, and heterogeneous IoT data. Classical RCA methods require partial or full knowledge of the system's dependency graph, which is rarely available in these complex networks. While federated learning (FL) offers a natural framework for decentralized settings, most existing FL methods assume homogeneous feature spaces and retrainable client models. These assumptions are not compatible with our problem setting. Different clients have different data features and often run fixed, proprietary models that cannot be modified. This paper presents a federated cross-client interdependency learning methodology for feature-partitioned, nonlinear time-series data, without requiring access to raw sensor streams or modifying proprietary client models. Each proprietary local client model is augmented with a Machine Learning (ML) model that encodes cross-client interdependencies. These ML models are coordinated via a global server that enforces representation consistency while preserving privacy through calibrated differential privacy noise. RCA is performed using model residuals and anomaly flags. We establish theoretical convergence guarantees and validate our approach on extensive simulations and a real-world industrial cybersecurity dataset."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Manuscript under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21928","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:36Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:09:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21927","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21927","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21927","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Hussain","name":"Hussain, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asmaa","familyName":"Abdallah","name":"Abdallah, Asmaa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdulkadir","familyName":"Celik","name":"Celik, Abdulkadir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emil","familyName":"Björnson","name":"Björnson, Emil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed M.","familyName":"Eltawil","name":"Eltawil, Ahmed M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Analyzing URA Geometry for Enhanced Near-Field Beamfocusing and Spatial Degrees of Freedom"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:04:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:53:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the deployment of large antenna arrays at high-frequency bands, future wireless communication systems are likely to operate in the radiative near-field. Unlike far-field beam steering, near-field beams can be focused on a spatial region with a finite depth, enabling spatial multiplexing in the range dimension. Moreover, in the line-of-sight MIMO near-field, multiple spatial degrees of freedom (DoF) are accessible, akin to a scattering- rich environment. In this paper, we derive the beamdepth for a generalized uniform rectangular array (URA) and investigate how the array geometry influences near-field beamdepth and its limits. We define the effective beamfocusing Rayleigh distance (EBRD), to present a near-field boundary with respect to beamfocusing and spatial multiplexing gains for the generalized URA. Our results demonstrate that under a fixed element count constraint, the array geometry has a strong impact on beamdepth, whereas this effect diminishes under a fixed aperture length constraint. Moreover, compared to uniform square arrays, elongated configurations such as uniform linear arrays (ULAs) yield narrower beamdepth and extend the effective near-field region defined by the EBRD. Building on these insights, we design a polar codebook for compressed-sensing-based channel estimation that leverages our findings. Simulation results show that the proposed polar codebook achieves a 2 dB NMSE improvement over state-of-the-art methods. Additionally, we present an analytical expression to quantify the effective spatial DoF in the near-field, revealing that they are also constrained by the EBRD. Notably, the maximum spatial DoF is achieved with a ULA configuration, outperforming a square URA in this regard."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21927","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:35Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:09:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21916","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21916","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21916","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunying","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Yunying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Kaczmarz methods for nearly singular linear systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:45:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:52:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Kaczmarz method is an efficient iterative algorithm for large-scale linear systems. However, its linear convergence rate suffers from ill-conditioned problems and is highly sensitive to the smallest nonzero singular value. In this work, we aim to extend the classical Kaczmarz to nearly singular linear systems that are row rank-deficient. We introduce a new concept of nearly singular property by treating the row space as an unstable subspace in the Grassman manifold. We then define a related important space called the approximate kernel, based on which a robust kernel-augmented Kaczmarz (KaK) is introduced via the subspace correction framework and analyzed by the well-known Xu--Zikatanov identity. To get an implementable version, we further introduce the approximate dual kernel and transform KaK into an equivalent kernel-augmented coordinate descent. Furthermore, we develop an accelerated variant and establish the improved rate of convergence matching the optimal complexity of first-order methods. Compared with existing methods, ours achieve uniform convergence rates for nearly singular linear systems, and the robustness has been confirmed by some numerical tests."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21916","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:19Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:09:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21899","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21899","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21899","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnau","familyName":"Romero","name":"Romero, Arnau","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carmen","familyName":"Delgado","name":"Delgado, Carmen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jana","familyName":"Baguer","name":"Baguer, Jana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raúl","familyName":"Suárez","name":"Suárez, Raúl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xavier","familyName":"Costa-Pérez","name":"Costa-Pérez, Xavier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Cellular-enabled Collaborative Robots Planning through GNSS data for SAR Scenarios"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:29:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:51:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.comcom.2025.108376","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cellular-enabled collaborative robots are becoming paramount in Search-and-Rescue (SAR) and emergency response. Crucially dependent on resilient mobile network connectivity, they serve as invaluable assets for tasks like rapid victim localization and the exploration of hazardous, otherwise unreachable areas. However, their reliance on battery power and the need for persistent, low-latency communication limit operational time and mobility. To address this, and considering the evolving capabilities of 5G/6G networks, we propose a novel SAR framework that includes Mission Planning and Mission Execution phases and that optimizes robot deployment. By considering parameters such as the exploration area size, terrain elevation, robot fleet size, communication-influenced energy profiles, desired exploration rate, and target response time, our framework determines the minimum number of robots required and their optimal paths to ensure effective coverage and timely data backhaul over mobile networks. Our results demonstrate the trade-offs between number of robots, explored area, and response time for wheeled and quadruped robots. Further, we quantify the impact of terrain elevation data on mission time and energy consumption, showing the benefits of incorporating real-world environmental factors that might also affect mobile signal propagation and connectivity into SAR planning. This framework provides critical insights for leveraging next-generation mobile networks to enhance autonomous SAR operations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"arXiv admin note: substantial text overlap with arXiv:2403.09177"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21899","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:08:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21892","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21892","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21892","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chandra","familyName":"Thapa","name":"Thapa, Chandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hajime","familyName":"Suzuki","name":"Suzuki, Hajime","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"APFuzz: Towards Automatic Greybox Protocol Fuzzing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:21:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:51:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Greybox protocol fuzzing is a random testing approach for stateful protocol implementations, where the input is protocol messages generated from mutations of seeds, and the search in the input space is driven by the feedback on coverage of both code and state. State model and message model are the core components of communication protocols, which also have significant impacts on protocol fuzzing. In this work, we propose APFuzz (Automatic greybox Protocol Fuzzer) with novel designs to increase the smartness of greybox protocol fuzzers from the perspectives of both the state model and the message model. On the one hand, APFuzz employs a two-stage process of static and dynamic analysis to automatically identify state variables, which are then used to infer an accurate state model during fuzzing. On the other hand, APFuzz introduces field-level mutation operations for binary protocols, leveraging message structure awareness enabled by Large Language Models. We conduct extensive experiments on a public protocol fuzzing benchmark, comparing APFuzz with the baseline fuzzer AFLNET as well as several state-of-the-art greybox protocol fuzzers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 4 figures, 9 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21892","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:44Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:08:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21873","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21873","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21873","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwei","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Shiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiashuo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiashuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihong","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Yihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GFPL: Generative Federated Prototype Learning for Resource-Constrained and Data-Imbalanced Vision Task"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:57:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:49:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated learning (FL) facilitates the secure utilization of decentralized images, advancing applications in medical image recognition and autonomous driving. However, conventional FL faces two critical challenges in real-world deployment: ineffective knowledge fusion caused by model updates biased toward majority-class features, and prohibitive communication overhead due to frequent transmissions of high-dimensional model parameters. Inspired by the human brain's efficiency in knowledge integration, we propose a novel Generative Federated Prototype Learning (GFPL) framework to address these issues. Within this framework, a prototype generation method based on Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM) captures the statistical information of class-wise features, while a prototype aggregation strategy using Bhattacharyya distance effectively fuses semantically similar knowledge across clients. In addition, these fused prototypes are leveraged to generate pseudo-features, thereby mitigating feature distribution imbalance across clients. To further enhance feature alignment during local training, we devise a dual-classifier architecture, optimized via a hybrid loss combining Dot Regression and Cross-Entropy. Extensive experiments on benchmarks show that GFPL improves model accuracy by 3.6% under imbalanced data settings while maintaining low communication cost."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21873","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:16Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:08:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21856","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21856","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21856","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaidi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kaidi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiguo","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Zhiguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel K. C.","familyName":"So","name":"So, Daniel K. C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leaky Coaxial Cable based Generalized Pinching-Antenna Systems with Dual-Port Feeding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:31:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"By leveraging the distributed leakage radiation of leaky coaxial cables (LCXs), the concept of pinching antennas can be generalized from the conventional high-frequency waveguide based architectures to cable based structures in lower-frequency scenarios. This paper investigates an LCX based generalized pinching-antenna system with dual-port feeding. By enabling bidirectional excitation along each cable, the proposed design significantly enhances spatial degrees of freedom. A comprehensive channel model is developed to characterize intra-cable attenuation, bidirectional phase progression, slot based radiation, and wireless propagation. Based on this model, both analog and hybrid beamforming frameworks are studied with the objective of maximizing the minimum achievable data rate. For analog transmission, slot activation, port selection, and power allocation are jointly optimized using matching theory, coalitional games, and bisection based power control. For hybrid transmission, zero-forcing (ZF) digital precoding is incorporated to eliminate inter-user interference, thereby simplifying slot activation and enabling closed-form optimal power allocation. Simulation results demonstrate that dual-port feeding provides notable performance gains over single-port LCX systems and fixed-antenna benchmarks, validating the effectiveness of the proposed beamforming and resource allocation designs under various transmit power levels and cable parameters."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21856","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:51Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21846","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21846","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21846","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masha","familyName":"Naslidnyk","name":"Naslidnyk, Masha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalable Kernel-Based Distances for Statistical Inference and Integration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:25:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Representing, comparing, and measuring the distance between probability distributions is a key task in computational statistics and machine learning. The choice of representation and the associated distance determine properties of the methods in which they are used: for example, certain distances can allow one to encode robustness or smoothness of the problem. Kernel methods offer flexible and rich Hilbert space representations of distributions that allow the modeller to enforce properties through the choice of kernel, and estimate associated distances at efficient nonparametric rates. In particular, the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD), a kernel-based distance constructed by comparing Hilbert space mean functions, has received significant attention due to its computational tractability and is favoured by practitioners.\n In this thesis, we conduct a thorough study of kernel-based distances with a focus on efficient computation, with core contributions in Chapters 3 to 6. Part I of the thesis is focused on the MMD, specifically on improved MMD estimation. In Chapter 3 we propose a theoretically sound, improved estimator for MMD in simulation-based inference. Then, in Chapter 4, we propose an MMD-based estimator for conditional expectations, a ubiquitous task in statistical computation. Closing Part I, in Chapter 5 we study the problem of calibration when MMD is applied to the task of integration.\n In Part II, motivated by the recent developments in kernel embeddings beyond the mean, we introduce a family of novel kernel-based discrepancies: kernel quantile discrepancies. These address some of the pitfalls of MMD, and are shown through both theoretical results and an empirical study to offer a competitive alternative to MMD and its fast approximations. We conclude with a discussion on broader lessons and future work emerging from the thesis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"PhD thesis"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21846","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:36Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21844","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21844","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21844","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruichen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ruichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying-Jun Angela","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ying-Jun Angela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianwei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JSAM: Privacy Straggler-Resilient Joint Client Selection and Incentive Mechanism Design in Differentially Private Federated Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:22:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Differentially private federated learning faces a fundamental tension: privacy protection mechanisms that safeguard client data simultaneously create quantifiable privacy costs that discourage participation, undermining the collaborative training process. Existing incentive mechanisms rely on unbiased client selection, forcing servers to compensate even the most privacy-sensitive clients (\"privacy stragglers\"), leading to systemic inefficiency and suboptimal resource allocation. We introduce JSAM (Joint client Selection and privacy compensAtion Mechanism), a Bayesian-optimal framework that simultaneously optimizes client selection probabilities and privacy compensation to maximize training effectiveness under budget constraints. Our approach transforms a complex 2N-dimensional optimization problem into an efficient three-dimensional formulation through novel theoretical characterization of optimal selection strategies. We prove that servers should preferentially select privacy-tolerant clients while excluding high-sensitivity participants, and uncover the counter-intuitive insight that clients with minimal privacy sensitivity may incur the highest cumulative costs due to frequent participation. Extensive evaluations on MNIST and CIFAR-10 demonstrate that JSAM achieves up to 15% improvement in test accuracy compared to existing unbiased selection mechanisms while maintaining cost efficiency across varying data heterogeneity levels."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21844","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:33Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21829","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21829","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21829","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Oliveira","name":"Oliveira, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Martins","familyName":"de Matos","name":"de Matos, David Martins","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StoryMovie: A Dataset for Semantic Alignment of Visual Stories with Movie Scripts and Subtitles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.10; I.4.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:01:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:47:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual storytelling models that correctly ground entities in images may still hallucinate semantic relationships, generating incorrect dialogue attribution, character interactions, or emotional states. We introduce StoryMovie, a dataset of 1,757 stories aligned with movie scripts and subtitles through LCS matching. Our alignment pipeline synchronizes screenplay dialogue with subtitle timestamps, enabling dialogue attribution by linking character names from scripts to temporal positions from subtitles. Using this aligned content, we generate stories that maintain visual grounding tags while incorporating authentic character names, dialogue, and relationship dynamics. We fine-tune Qwen Storyteller3 on this dataset, building on prior work in visual grounding and entity re-identification. Evaluation using DeepSeek V3 as judge shows that Storyteller3 achieves an 89.9% win rate against base Qwen2.5-VL 7B on subtitle alignment. Compared to Storyteller, trained without script grounding,\n Storyteller3 achieves 48.5% versus 38.0%, confirming that semantic alignment progressively improves dialogue attribution beyond visual grounding alone."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, submitted to Journal of Visual Communication and Image Representation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21829","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21793","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21793","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21793","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Teng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiandong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadali","familyName":"Mohammadi","name":"Mohammadi, Mohammadali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hien Quoc","familyName":"Ngo","name":"Ngo, Hien Quoc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michail","familyName":"Matthaiou","name":"Matthaiou, Michail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Availability of Aerial Heterogeneous Networks for Reliable Emergency Communications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:17:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:44:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate network availability (NA) in aerial heterogeneous networks (AHetNets) for effective emergency rescue, where diverse delay-constrained communication services must be provided to user equipments (UEs) with varying mobility. The heterogeneity in delay constraints and UE mobility introduces resource allocation conflicts and imbalances, which undermine communication reliability and challenge NA. Although unified resource allocation (URA) can mitigate these issues, it remains unclear whether NA can be sustained under such diverse conditions. To address this, we derive expressions for the lower bound (LB) on NA in AHetNets under URA. Our analysis reveals that extended heterogeneity significantly degrades the LB due to resource limitations-even when the heterogeneity stems from additional services under less stringent delay constraints (LSDC) or from UEs with lower mobility. To overcome this degradation, we formulate and solve a joint optimization problem for the number of UEs sharing time-frequency resources ($K$) and pilot length ($ξ$), aiming to enhance the LB by improving spatial, frequency, and temporal resource efficiency. Simulation results validate our analysis and demonstrate that jointly optimizing $K$ and $ξ$ enables AHetNets to achieve the target NA under greater heterogeneity, outperforming existing resource allocation policies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21793","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:19Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:06:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21780","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21780","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21780","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zunhai","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zunhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hansen","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Hansen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyu","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Keyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Dahai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengwu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhengwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngai","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Ngai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"XStreamVGGT: Extremely Memory-Efficient Streaming Vision Geometry Grounded Transformer with KV Cache Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:02:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:44:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning-based 3D visual geometry models have significantly advanced with the advent of large-scale transformers. Among these, StreamVGGT leverages frame-wise causal attention to deliver robust and efficient streaming 3D reconstruction. However, it suffers from unbounded growth in the Key-Value (KV) cache due to the massive influx of vision tokens from multi-image and long-video inputs, leading to increased memory consumption and inference latency as input frames accumulate. This ultimately limits its scalability for long-horizon applications. To address this gap, we propose XStreamVGGT, a tuning-free approach that seamlessly integrates pruning and quantization to systematically compress the KV cache, enabling extremely memory-efficient streaming inference. Specifically, redundant KVs generated from multi-frame inputs are initially pruned to conform to a fixed KV memory budget using an efficient token-importance identification mechanism that maintains full compatibility with high-performance attention kernels (e.g., FlashAttention). Additionally, leveraging the inherent distribution patterns of KV tensors, we apply dimension-adaptive KV quantization within the pruning pipeline to further minimize memory overhead while preserving numerical accuracy. Extensive evaluations show that XStreamVGGT achieves mostly negligible performance degradation while substantially reducing memory usage by 4.42$\\times$ and accelerating inference by 5.48$\\times$, enabling practical and scalable streaming 3D applications. The code is available at https://github.com/ywh187/XStreamVGGT/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submission to the Journal of the Society for Information Display"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21780","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:59Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:06:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21771","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21771","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21771","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph Albert","familyName":"Johns","name":"Johns, Christoph Albert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"László","familyName":"Kopácsi","name":"Kopácsi, László","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Barz","name":"Barz, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Sonntag","name":"Sonntag, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Heads Up!: Towards In Situ Photogrammetry Annotations and Augmented Reality Visualizations for Guided Backcountry Skiing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:42:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:42:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Backcountry skiing is an activity where a group of skiers navigate challenging environmental conditions to ski outside of managed areas. This activity requires careful monitoring and effective communication around the current weather and terrain conditions to ensure skier safety. We aim to support and facilitate this communication by providing backcountry guides with a set of in situ spatial annotation tools to communicate hazards and appropriate speeds to the ski recreationalists. A guide can use a tablet application to annotate a photogrammetry-based map of a mountainside, for example, one collected using a commercial camera drone, with hazard points, slow-down zones, and safe zones. These annotations are communicated to the skiers via visual overlays in augmented reality heads-up displays. We present a prototype consisting of a web application and a virtual reality display that mirror the guide's and skier's perspectives, enabling participatory interaction design studies in a safe environment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at AlpCHI 2026 Demos, March 01-05, 2026, Ascona, Switzerland"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21771","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:46Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21767","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21767","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21767","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Giesl","name":"Giesl, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Hafstein","name":"Hafstein, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"B.","familyName":"Hamzi","name":"Hamzi, B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Owhadi","name":"Owhadi, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Santin","name":"Santin, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"U.","familyName":"Vaidya","name":"Vaidya, U.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kernel Methods for the Construction of Certified Lyapunov Functions via Approximate Koopman Eigenfunctions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:39:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:42:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a kernel-based methodology for constructing Lyapunov functions for nonlinear dynamical systems using approximate Koopman eigenfunctions. Our approach decomposes principal Koopman eigenfunctions into linear and nonlinear components, where the linear part is obtained from the system's linearization and the nonlinear part is computed by solving a partial differential equation using symmetric kernel collocation in reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces (RKHS). The resulting Lyapunov function is constructed as a quadratic form in the approximate eigenfunctions. We establish error bounds relating the approximation quality to the fill distance of collocation points and provide a certification procedure using continuous piecewise affine (CPA) methods. Numerical experiments on benchmark systems, including a polynomial system and the Duffing oscillator, demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21767","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21760","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21760","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21760","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Euisoo","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Euisoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byunghyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Byunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunjin","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyunjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seonghye","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Seonghye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jae-Gil","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jae-Gil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Accelerating Diffusion via Hybrid Data-Pipeline Parallelism Based on Conditional Guidance Scheduling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:23:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:41:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have achieved remarkable progress in high-fidelity image, video, and audio generation, yet inference remains computationally expensive. Nevertheless, current diffusion acceleration methods based on distributed parallelism suffer from noticeable generation artifacts and fail to achieve substantial acceleration proportional to the number of GPUs. Therefore, we propose a hybrid parallelism framework that combines a novel data parallel strategy, condition-based partitioning, with an optimal pipeline scheduling method, adaptive parallelism switching, to reduce generation latency and achieve high generation quality in conditional diffusion models. The key ideas are to (i) leverage the conditional and unconditional denoising paths as a new data-partitioning perspective and (ii) adaptively enable optimal pipeline parallelism according to the denoising discrepancy between these two paths. Our framework achieves $2.31\\times$ and $2.07\\times$ latency reductions on SDXL and SD3, respectively, using two NVIDIA RTX~3090 GPUs, while preserving image quality. This result confirms the generality of our approach across U-Net-based diffusion models and DiT-based flow-matching architectures. Our approach also outperforms existing methods in acceleration under high-resolution synthesis settings. Code is available at https://github.com/kaist-dmlab/Hybridiff."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21760","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:30Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21758","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21758","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21758","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ori","familyName":"Hendler","name":"Hendler, Ori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ronen","familyName":"Segev","name":"Segev, Ronen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maoz","familyName":"Shamir","name":"Shamir, Maoz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Limits of optimal decoding under synaptic coarse-tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biological Physics (physics.bio-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:20:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:41:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sensory information propagates through successive processing stages in the brain, where synaptic weight patterns between stations determine how downstream neurons decode information from upstream populations. Although optimized synaptic connectivity can enhance information transmission, it requires precise weight tuning. Recent evidence depicting substantial synaptic volatility raises two fundamental questions: How does coarse-tuning of synaptic connectivity affect information transmission? What strategies could the nervous system employ to maintain reliable communication despite synaptic fluctuations? We addressed these questions by analyzing the signal-to-noise ratio ($SNR$) for binary stimulus discrimination under two decoding schemes: a naive population average and an optimized linear decoder. For the naive decoder, we found that $SNR$ remains largely insensitive to synaptic imprecision, since performance is already limited by correlated noise in neuronal responses. For the optimal decoder, we identified three distinct regimes. Under weak coarse-tuning, $SNR^2$ scales linearly with population size $N$. Under moderate coarse-tuning, scaling becomes sublinear. Under strong coarse-tuning, the regime most consistent with observed neuronal heterogeneity, $SNR$ saturates and can not be improved by recruiting larger populations. This limitation persists even when incorporating feedforward or recurrent network architectures. These findings suggest that in the biologically relevant regime of strong coarse-tuning, naive and optimal decoders can achieve qualitatively similar performance. The analysis shows that effective readout under synaptic volatility is constrained to an invariant low-dimensional manifold aligned with the naive decoder, potentially pointing to a fundamental principle for robust neural computation in the face of ongoing synaptic remodeling."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21758","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:27Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21744","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21744","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21744","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boxuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Boxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lauri","familyName":"Mela","name":"Mela, Lauri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexis A.","familyName":"Dowhuszko","name":"Dowhuszko, Alexis A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kalle","familyName":"Ruttik","name":"Ruttik, Kalle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riku","familyName":"Jäntti","name":"Jäntti, Riku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dual-Hop Joint Visible Light and Backscatter Communication Relaying under Finite Blocklength"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:55:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:40:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates a dual-hop joint visible light communication (VLC) and backscatter communication (BC) relaying framework under the finite blocklength (FBL) constraint, aiming at energy-neutral Ambient Internet of Things (A-IoT) deployments. In the proposed system, indoor LED access points are used to simultaneously provide illumination and transmit information over light to a backscatter device (BD), which harvests optical energy and backscatters the received messages to user equipments (UEs) equipped with radio frequency (RF) front ends. This forwarding of the information from VLC to RF channels is implemented without the need for carrier synthesizers and power amplifiers at the IoT node. By modeling the end-to-end communication link with short-packet IoT traffic and realistic levels of interference between adjacent VLC coverage areas, we analyze the outage performance and achievable data rate of the proposed system. Simulation results demonstrate that key factors, such as placement and orientation of the BD, as well as the selected code rate of the system affect reliability and data rate that can be achieved for communication purposes. The insights gained from this study pave the way for ambient power-enabled IoT solutions and future hybrid VLC/RF network designs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 10 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21744","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:06Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21730","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21730","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21730","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Borrill","name":"Borrill, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lamport's Arrow of Time: The Category Mistake in Logical Clocks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:39:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:39:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Lamport's 1978 paper introduced the happens-before relation and logical clocks, freeing distributed systems from dependence on synchronized physical clocks. This is widely understood as a move away from Newtonian absolute time. We argue that Lamport's formalism retains a deeper and largely unexamined assumption: that causality induces a globally well-defined directed acyclic graph (DAG) over events -- a forward-in-time-only (FITO) structure that functions as an arrow of time embedded at the semantic level. Following Ryle's analysis of category mistakes, we show that this assumption conflates an epistemic construct (the logical ordering of messages) with an ontic claim (that physical causality is globally acyclic and monotonic). We trace this conflation through Shannon's channel model, TLA+, Bell's theorem, and the impossibility results of Fischer-Lynch-Paterson and Brewer's CAP theorem. We then show that special and general relativity permit only local causal structure, and that recent work on indefinite causal order demonstrates that nature admits correlations with no well-defined causal ordering. We propose that mutual information conservation, rather than temporal precedence, provides a more fundamental primitive for distributed consistency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 32 references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21730","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21724","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21724","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21724","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Myriam","familyName":"Rihani","name":"Rihani, Myriam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristina","familyName":"Martinez-Oliver","name":"Martinez-Oliver, Cristina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus A.","familyName":"Scherrer","name":"Scherrer, Markus A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heinz","familyName":"Schmid","name":"Schmid, Heinz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kirsten E.","familyName":"Moselund","name":"Moselund, Kirsten E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Iadanza","name":"Iadanza, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trap-dependent current suppression of optically excited III-V nanowires at cryogenic temperatures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:33:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:38:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The advancement of quantum technology networks necessitates high-speed, low-thermal load, and minimal-noise communication links between cryogenic and room-temperature components. At the heart of modern telecommunication, lay optical interconnects allowing for large data transfer capabilities via optical fibers. However, cryogenic photonic technologies remain largely unexplored and require a detailed understanding of material behavior and defect dynamics at low temperatures. In this work, we present the first comprehensive study of integrated III-V heterostructures operating at cryogenic temperatures down to 5K. Using an integrated n-InP/i-InGaAs/p-InP/p-InGaAs stack monolithically grown on silicon, we identify a temperature-dependent current-lowering mechanism arising from trap states becoming increasingly active below 140K. We demonstrate for the first time that these traps can be equivalently excited and controlled through either thermal or optical energy, revealing a dual modulation mechanism. These findings provide new insights into carrier transport and defect behavior in III-V heterostructures at cryogenic temperatures, advancing the field of cryogenic photonics and offering a non-destructive approach for identifying and characterizing material impurities in integrated quantum and optoelectronic devices."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21724","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:36Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21715","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21715","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21715","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhui","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenchuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Two-Stage Active Distribution Network Voltage Control via LLM-RL Collaboration: A Hybrid Knowledge-Data-Driven Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:22:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:37:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The growing integration of distributed photovoltaics (PVs) into active distribution networks (ADNs) has exacerbated operational challenges, making it imperative to coordinate diverse equipment to mitigate voltage violations and enhance power quality. Although existing data-driven approaches have demonstrated effectiveness in the voltage control problem, they often require extensive trial-and-error exploration and struggle to incorporate heterogeneous information, such as day-ahead forecasts and semantic-based grid codes. Considering the operational scenarios and requirements in real-world ADNs, in this paper, we propose a hybrid knowledge-data-driven approach that leverages dynamic collaboration between a large language model (LLM) agent and a reinforcement learning (RL) agent to achieve two-stage voltage control. In the day-ahead stage, the LLM agent receives coarse region-level forecasts and generates scheduling strategies for on-load tap changer (OLTC) and shunt capacitors (SCs) to regulate the overall voltage profile. Then in the intra-day stage, based on accurate node-level measurements, the RL agent refines terminal voltages by deriving reactive power generation strategies for PV inverters. On top of the LLM-RL collaboration framework, we further propose a self-evolution mechanism for the LLM agent and a pretrain-finetune pipeline for the RL agent, effectively enhancing and coordinating the policies for both agents. The proposed approach not only aligns more closely with practical operational characteristics but also effectively utilizes the inherent knowledge and reasoning capabilities of the LLM agent, significantly improving training efficiency and voltage control performance. Comprehensive comparisons and ablation studies demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21715","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:23Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21656","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21656","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21656","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saifollah","familyName":"Rasouli","name":"Rasouli, Saifollah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Somaye","familyName":"Fathollazade","name":"Fathollazade, Somaye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Mohammadi","name":"Mohammadi, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pure-amplitude holograms for high-efficiency generation of phase radial grating based radial carpet beams: Theory and experiments under plane-wave and Gaussian illumination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:34:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study introduces a pure-amplitude hologram (PAH) for generating radial carpet beams (RCBs), which are conventionally produced using pure phase radial gratings (PRGs). The hologram is designed by embedding the transmission function of a binary PRG into the phase argument of the cosine term(s) of an amplitude linear grating. When illuminated with a plane wave, this hologram generates RCBs in the non-zero diffraction orders, and when illuminated with a Gaussian beam, it generates RCB-like patterns at specific propagation distances. This method entirely eliminates the need for complex and expensive spatial light modulators (SLMs). The study presents a theory of diffraction for plane and Gaussian beams from such holograms, including a specific theoretical treatment of Gaussian beam diffraction from PRGs. Through theoretical analysis and experiments, we demonstrate how different RCBs can be generated at different diffraction orders due to the phase-amplitude enhancement resulting from multiplying the diffraction order number by the phase amplitude of the embedded base PRG, when the illuminating beam is a plane wave. For the Gaussian beam case, we show how different RCB-like patterns can be generated at different diffraction orders for the same reason, though only at specific propagation distances. Experimental and numerical results indicate that this technique yields RCBs and RCB-like patterns with approximately five times the useful power of their SLM-generated counterparts, demonstrating significantly higher power efficiency. This advantage renders the proposed method highly suitable for applications such as multiple optical trapping and free-space optical communication."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages and 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21656","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:50Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21638","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21638","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21638","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Anqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruihan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ruihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenzhong","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Zhenzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-dimensional Assessment and Explainable Feedback for Counselor Responses to Client Resistance in Text-based Counseling with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:05:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:31:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effectively addressing client resistance is a sophisticated clinical skill in psychological counseling, yet practitioners often lack timely and scalable supervisory feedback to refine their approaches. Although current NLP research has examined overall counseling quality and general therapeutic skills, it fails to provide granular evaluations of high-stakes moments where clients exhibit resistance. In this work, we present a comprehensive pipeline for the multi-dimensional evaluation of human counselors' interventions specifically targeting client resistance in text-based therapy. We introduce a theory-driven framework that decomposes counselor responses into four distinct communication mechanisms. Leveraging this framework, we curate and share an expert-annotated dataset of real-world counseling excerpts, pairing counselor-client interactions with professional ratings and explanatory rationales. Using this data, we perform full-parameter instruction tuning on a Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct backbone to model fine-grained evaluative judgments of response quality and generate explanations underlying. Experimental results show that our approach can effectively distinguish the quality of different communication mechanisms (77-81% F1), substantially outperforming GPT-4o and Claude-3.5-Sonnet (45-59% F1). Moreover, the model produces high-quality explanations that closely align with expert references and receive near-ceiling ratings from human experts (2.8-2.9/3.0). A controlled experiment with 43 counselors further confirms that receiving these AI-generated feedback significantly improves counselors' ability to respond effectively to client resistance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21638","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21635","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21635","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21635","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Galla","name":"Galla, Tobias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A diffusion approximation for systems with frequent weak resetting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:58:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a diffusion approximation for systems subject to fast random resetting by small amplitudes. Equivalently, this describes systems with frequent but small catastrophes. We demonstrate the validity of the approximation by computing the stationary distribution and mean first-passage times of simple one-dimensional systems. The approximation captures dynamically induced correlations in multi-particle systems, and it can be used to generalise the conditionally independent and identically distributed structure recently found in systems with full resetting. Finally, we show that resetting can induce cycles and patterns, which can be characterised using the diffusion approximation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6+2+12 pages, 5+6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21635","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:15Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21630","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21630","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21630","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Bertoni","name":"Bertoni, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saverio","familyName":"Giallorenzo","name":"Giallorenzo, Saverio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Peressotti","name":"Peressotti, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Type-Based Enforcement of Non-Interference for Choreographic Programming"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:50:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Choreographies describe distributed protocols from a global viewpoint, enabling correct-by-construction synthesis of local behaviours. We develop a policy-parametric type system that prevents information leaks from high-security data to low-security observers, handling both explicit and implicit flows through a program-counter discipline. The system supports recursive procedures via a procedure context that we reconstruct through constraint generation. We prove termination-insensitive non-interference with respect to a standard small-step semantics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21630","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:07Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21609","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21609","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21609","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huimin","familyName":"Lao","name":"Lao, Huimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"San","familyName":"Ling","name":"Ling, San","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Concatenated Sum-Rank Codes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:12:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:29:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sum-rank codes have wide applications in multishot network coding, distributed storage and the construction of space-time codes. Asymptotically good sequences of linearized algebraic geometry sum-rank codes, exceeding the Gilbert-Varshamov-like bound, were constructed in a recent paper published in IEEE Trans. Inf. Theory by E. Berardini and X. Caruso. We call this bound the Tsfasman-Vladut-Zink-like bound. In this paper, we introduce the concatenation of a sum-rank code and a Hamming metric code. Then many sum-rank codes with good parameters, which are better than sum-rank BCH codes, are constructed simply and explicitly. Moreover, we obtain an asymptotically good sequence of sum-rank codes exceeding the Tsfasman-Vladut-Zink-like bound and the Gilbert-Varshamov-like bound."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21609","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:33Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21600","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21600","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21600","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ganap Ashit","familyName":"Tewary","name":"Tewary, Ganap Ashit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nrusinga Charan","familyName":"Gantayat","name":"Gantayat, Nrusinga Charan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeff","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jeff","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AQR-HNSW: Accelerating Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search via Density-aware Quantization and Multi-stage Re-ranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:58:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:28:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search has become fundamental to modern AI infrastructure, powering recommendation systems, search engines, and large language models across industry leaders from Google to OpenAI. Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) graphs have emerged as the dominant ANN algorithm, widely adopted in production systems due to their superior recall versus latency balance. However, as vector databases scale to billions of embeddings, HNSW faces critical bottlenecks: memory consumption expands, distance computation overhead dominates query latency, and it suffers suboptimal performance on heterogeneous data distributions. This paper presents Adaptive Quantization and Rerank HNSW (AQR-HNSW), a novel framework that synergistically integrates three strategies to enhance HNSW scalability. AQR-HNSW introduces (1) density-aware adaptive quantization, achieving 4x compression while preserving distance relationships; (2) multi-state re-ranking that reduces unnecessary computations by 35%; and (3) quantization-optimized SIMD implementations delivering 16-64 operations per cycle across architectures. Evaluation on standard benchmarks demonstrates 2.5-3.3x higher queries per second (QPS) than state-of-the-art HNSW implementations while maintaining over 98% recall, with 75% memory reduction for the index graph and 5x faster index construction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at DAC 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21600","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:18Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21597","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21597","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21597","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongwei","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisen","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong Ting","familyName":"Tsang","name":"Tsang, Hong Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqiu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NGDB-Zoo: Towards Efficient and Scalable Neural Graph Databases Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:46:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:27:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural Graph Databases (NGDBs) facilitate complex logical reasoning over incomplete knowledge structures, yet their training efficiency and expressivity are constrained by rigid query-level batching and structure-exclusive embeddings. We present NGDB-Zoo, a unified framework that resolves these bottlenecks by synergizing operator-level training with semantic augmentation. By decoupling logical operators from query topologies, NGDB-Zoo transforms the training loop into a dynamically scheduled data-flow execution, enabling multi-stream parallelism and achieving a $1.8\\times$ - $6.8\\times$ throughput compared to baselines. Furthermore, we formalize a decoupled architecture to integrate high-dimensional semantic priors from Pre-trained Text Encoders (PTEs) without triggering I/O stalls or memory overflows. Extensive evaluations on six benchmarks, including massive graphs like ogbl-wikikg2 and ATLAS-Wiki, demonstrate that NGDB-Zoo maintains high GPU utilization across diverse logical patterns and significantly mitigates representation friction in hybrid neuro-symbolic reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21597","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:14Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21568","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21568","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21568","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuvraj","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Yuvraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pallav","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Pallav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Ad-Hoc Scripts to Orchestrated Pipelines: Architecting a Resilient ELT Framework for Developer Productivity Metrics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:46:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:25:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.33144/24545414/ijit-v12i1p2","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Developer Productivity Dashboards are essential for visualizing DevOps performance metrics such as Deployment Frequency and Change Failure Rate (DORA). However, the utility of these dashboards is frequently undermined by data reliability issues. In early iterations of our platform, ad-hoc ingestion scripts (Cron jobs) led to \"silent failures,\" where data gaps went undetected for days, eroding organizational trust. This paper reports on our experience migrating from legacy scheduling to a robust Extract-Load-Transform (ELT) pipeline using Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) orchestration and Medallion Architecture. We detail the operational benefits of decoupling data extraction from transformation, the necessity of immutable raw history for metric redefinition, and the implementation of state-based dependency management. Our experience suggests that treating the metrics pipeline as a production-grade distributed system is a prerequisite for sustainable engineering analytics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21568","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:28Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:00:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21563","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21563","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21563","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sewon","familyName":"Jeong","name":"Jeong, Sewon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shrobona","familyName":"Bagchi","name":"Bagchi, Shrobona","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehak","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaehak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyang-Tag","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Hyang-Tag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong-Su","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yong-Su","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taeyoung","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Taeyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seung-Woo","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Seung-Woo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Entanglement recovery by reversing the effect of noise in quantum repeater"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:37:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:25:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a method to directly recover the degree of entanglement distributed by entanglement swapping in the presence of noise. Our approach introduces a reversing operation that probabilistically undoes the effect of amplitude damping or photon loss on a single entangled pair, enabling heralded recovery of entanglement. We demonstrate that entanglement can be substantially recovered even under strong noise, including parameter regimes where the distributed entanglement would otherwise vanish due to entanglement sudden death. We analyze the effectiveness of the protocol in two representative repeater models, i.e.,~two-way and one-way architectures and identify the optimal reversing strategy. Due to its heralded and single-copy nature, our protocol is readily compatible with other entanglement recovery techniques such as entanglement purification and distillation. Our work provides a practical and experimentally feasible way toward robust entanglement distribution in current and near-term quantum repeater architectures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21563","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:00:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21560","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21560","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21560","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gourab","familyName":"Giri","name":"Giri, Gourab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Mignone","name":"Mignone, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Suriano","name":"Suriano, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Rossazza","name":"Rossazza, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Truzzi","name":"Truzzi, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Performance assessment of the gPLUTO code for the numerical modeling of radio galaxy evolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:27:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:24:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-resolution tri-axial simulations are indispensable for realistically co-modeling the dynamical signatures and the radiative fingerprints of astrophysical jets, which are becoming increasingly important in modern computational studies of jet physics. However, such simulations impose extreme computational requirements that often exceed the capabilities of conventional CPU-based codes. GPU-accelerated simulations offer a transformative solution to mitigate these limitations. In this work, we present a detailed performance benchmarking of the recently developed GPU-enabled PLUTO code (gPLUTO), demonstrating runtime speed-ups ranging from an order of magnitude to (approximately) over 30 relative to CPU-only configurations. A direct comparison between computations of extragalactic jet propagation performed at different grid resolutions confirm the physical fidelity and production readiness of the gPLUTO code, while underscoring the importance of resolving the jet radius adequately to capture the jet dynamics accurately. Leveraging GPU-PLUTO's capabilities, we finally present an application by performing high-resolution simulations of giant radio galaxy jets (GRGs $\\gtrsim 1$ Mpc), representing the first such well-resolved 3D study to our knowledge (resolving scales down to 500 pc). These simulations probe a range of environmental effects on GRG jets, clarifying their formation from central galaxies within host cosmic structures, rapid peripheral expansion, and the development of asymmetric cocoon morphologies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication: Astronomy and Computing (Special issue: HPC in Cosmology and Astrophysics)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21560","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:15Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:00:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21558","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21558","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21558","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifeng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zhifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salman","familyName":"Durrani","name":"Durrani, Salman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiangyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josep Miquel","familyName":"Jornet","name":"Jornet, Josep Miquel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markku","familyName":"Juntti","name":"Juntti, Markku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Impact of Pointing Errors and Correlated Wall Blockages on Practical Grid-based Indoor Terahertz Communication Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:25:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:24:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Terahertz (THz) communications has emerged as a promising technology for future wireless systems due to its potential to support extremely high data rates. However, severe path loss, blockage effects, and sensitivity to beam misalignment pose major challenges to reliable indoor THz communications. In this paper, we investigate the coverage probability of downlink transmission in a three-dimensional (3D) indoor THz communication system under structured access point (AP) deployments, with a focus on square and hexagonal grid topologies. A tractable analytical framework is developed to jointly account for human blockages, correlated wall blockages across APs, beam training, and residual pointing error. Numerical results demonstrate that wall blockage correlation significantly reduces the association and coverage probabilities, and its impact cannot be neglected in system performance analysis. Compared with square grid AP deployments, hexagonal grids consistently achieve higher coverage by mitigating correlated wall blockage effects and reducing the distances between user equipments (UEs) and their associated APs. Furthermore, coverage performance is shown to strongly depend on the UE location, with noticeable degradation as the UE moves away from its nearest AP. Residual pointing error is found to introduce substantial coverage loss, especially for longer links. In addition, beam training analysis reveals a non-monotonic relationship between antenna array size and training overhead, highlighting an inherent tradeoff among antenna configuration, beamwidth selection, and beam training efficiency. These findings provide useful insights into the design and deployment of practical indoor THz communication systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21558","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:13Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:00:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21528","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21528","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21528","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Akrout","name":"Akrout, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amine","familyName":"Mezghani","name":"Mezghani, Amine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faouzi","familyName":"Bellili","name":"Bellili, Faouzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert W.","familyName":"Heath","name":"Heath, Robert W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Guided Wireless Technology for Near-Field Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:30:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:21:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Guided wireless technology is an innovative approach that combines the strengths of guided waves and wireless communication. In traditional wireless systems, signals propagate through the air, where they are vulnerable to interference, attenuation, and jamming. Guided communication, in contrast, confines signals within a physical medium, significantly reducing interference and supporting higher data rates over longer distances. Guided wireless technology harnesses these benefits by creating guided wireless channels and offering a controlled pathway for electromagnetic waves. This work harnesses these benefits by focusing on the modeling of near-field communication through long connected arrays deployed in linear-cell environments. We derive a circuit model for long array as an infinitely long dipole with multiple periodic feed points before approximating it with a finite array through open circuiting. Through our simulations, we show how the standing wave phenomenon is confirmed by the oscillations in spectral efficiency. We also demonstrate the capability of the LMMSE transmit beamformer in mitigating interference and minimizing the mean square error by adaptively allocating more power to the user experiencing the most severe channel attenuation, resulting in a more balanced variation of achievable rates across users."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the IEEE Asilomar Conference on Signals, Systems \u0026amp; Computers 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21528","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:28Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21481","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21481","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21481","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohui","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Awareness to Application: Strengthening Recruitment for NSF S-STEM Scholarships in Computer Science"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:14:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:15:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recruiting academically strong students into NSF S-STEM scholarship programs remains a persistent challenge in computer science education. This paper presents the design and initial implementation of a suite of targeted recruitment strategies for our NSF-funded project. Our recruitment strategy leverages multiple channels. Information sessions and early outreach efforts were employed to increase awareness and reduce perceived barriers to applying. Data from our recruitment includes applicant demographics, academic performance, financial aid profiles, recruitment source tracking, and survey responses on students awareness and decision-making processes. These data provide a foundation for evaluating the reach and effectiveness of various recruitment strategies and identifying factors that influence student application decisions. Quantitative and qualitative research approaches are employed to examine the implementation and outcomes of proactive recruitment strategies. Our preliminary analysis indicates that direct information sessions and departmental emails are effective recruitment strategies, accounting for a large portion of eligible applications. Our findings emphasize the importance of early communication about the program, clearly defined eligibility criteria, and a streamlined application process. By sharing ongoing progress and lessons learned from our project, this paper contributes evidence-based insights into recruitment practices and offers strategies that can be adapted by other institutions implementing NSF S-STEM programs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21481","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21477","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21477","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21477","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengding","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhengding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaifeng","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zaifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prabhleen","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Prabhleen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vibha","familyName":"Murthy","name":"Murthy, Vibha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongkai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Swanson","name":"Swanson, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pancake: Hierarchical Memory System for Multi-Agent LLM Serving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:09:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:14:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we identify and address the core challenges of agentic memory management in LLM serving, where large-scale storage, frequent updates, and multiple coexisting agents jointly introduce complex and high-cost approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) searching problems. We present Pancake, a multi-tier agentic memory system that unifies three key techniques: (i) multi-level index caching for single agents, (ii) coordinated index management across multiple agents, and (iii) collaborative GPU-CPU acceleration. Pancake exposes easy-to-use interface that can be integrated into memory-based agents like Mem-GPT, and is compatible with agentic frameworks such as LangChain and LlamaIndex. Experiments on realistic agent workloads show that Pancake substantially outperforms existing frameworks, achieving more than 4.29x end-to-end throughput improvement."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21477","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21472","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21472","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21472","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louis","familyName":"Bethune","name":"Bethune, Louis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Turrisi","name":"Turrisi, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno Kacper","familyName":"Mlodozeniec","name":"Mlodozeniec, Bruno Kacper","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pau Rodriguez","familyName":"Lopez","name":"Lopez, Pau Rodriguez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lokesh","familyName":"Boominathan","name":"Boominathan, Lokesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikhil","familyName":"Bhendawade","name":"Bhendawade, Nikhil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amitis","familyName":"Shidani","name":"Shidani, Amitis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joris","familyName":"Pelemans","name":"Pelemans, Joris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theo X.","familyName":"Olausson","name":"Olausson, Theo X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devon","familyName":"Hjelm","name":"Hjelm, Devon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Dixon","name":"Dixon, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joao","familyName":"Monteiro","name":"Monteiro, Joao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierre","familyName":"Ablin","name":"Ablin, Pierre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishnu","familyName":"Banna","name":"Banna, Vishnu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arno","familyName":"Blaas","name":"Blaas, Arno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Henderson","name":"Henderson, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kari","familyName":"Noriy","name":"Noriy, Kari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Busbridge","name":"Busbridge, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josh","familyName":"Susskind","name":"Susskind, Josh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Cuturi","name":"Cuturi, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irina","familyName":"Belousova","name":"Belousova, Irina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Zappella","name":"Zappella, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Russ","familyName":"Webb","name":"Webb, Russ","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Ramapuram","name":"Ramapuram, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Design Space of Tri-Modal Masked Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:02:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:14:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Discrete diffusion models have emerged as strong alternatives to autoregressive language models, with recent work initializing and fine-tuning a base unimodal model for bimodal generation. Diverging from previous approaches, we introduce the first tri-modal masked diffusion model pretrained from scratch on text, image-text, and audio-text data. We systematically analyze multimodal scaling laws, modality mixing ratios, noise schedules, and batch-size effects, and we provide optimized inference sampling defaults. Our batch-size analysis yields a novel stochastic differential equation (SDE)-based reparameterization that eliminates the need for tuning the optimal batch size as reported in recent work. This reparameterization decouples the physical batch size, often chosen based on compute constraints (GPU saturation, FLOP efficiency, wall-clock time), from the logical batch size, chosen to balance gradient variance during stochastic optimization. Finally, we pretrain a preliminary 3B-parameter tri-modal model on 6.4T tokens, demonstrating the capabilities of a unified design and achieving strong results in text generation, text-to-image tasks, and text-to-speech tasks. Our work represents the largest-scale systematic open study of multimodal discrete diffusion models conducted to date, providing insights into scaling behaviors across multiple modalities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"41 pages, 29 figures, 10 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21472","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:04Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21470","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21470","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21470","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fedor","familyName":"Sandomirskiy","name":"Sandomirskiy, Fedor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Wincelberg","name":"Wincelberg, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Delegation in Strategic Environments and Equilibrium Uniqueness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:48:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:14:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We ask when a normal-form game yields a single equilibrium prediction, even if players can coordinate by delegating play to an intermediary such as a platform or a cartel. Delegation outcomes are modeled via coarse correlated equilibria (CCE) when the intermediary cannot punish deviators, and via the set of individually rational correlated profiles (IRCP) when it can. We characterize games in which the IRCP or the CCE is unique, uncovering a structural link between these solution concepts. Our analysis also provides new conditions for the uniqueness of classical correlated and Nash equilibria that do not rely on the existence of dominant strategies. The resulting equilibria are robust to players' information about the environment, payoff perturbations, pre-play communication, equilibrium selection, and learning dynamics. We apply these results to collusion-proof mechanism design."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21470","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:02Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Van-Chung","familyName":"Luu","name":"Luu, Van-Chung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toan-Van","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Toan-Van","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuria","familyName":"González-Prelcic","name":"González-Prelcic, Nuria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duy H. N.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Duy H. N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semi-Gridless Variational Bayes Channel Estimation in XL-MIMO: Near-Field Modeling and Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:04:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Extremely large antenna arrays and high-frequency operation are two key technologies that advance performance metrics such as higher data rates, lower latency, and wider coverage in sixth-generation communications. However, the adoption of these technologies fundamentally changes the characteristics of wavefronts, forcing communication systems to operate in the near-field region. The transition from planar far-field communications to spherical near-field propagation necessitates novel channel estimation algorithms to fully exploit the unique features of spherical wavefronts for advanced transceiver design. To this end, we propose a novel semi-gridless channel estimation approach based on a variational Bayesian (VB) inference framework. Specifically, we reformulate the near-field channel model for both uniform linear arrays and uniform planar arrays into separate direction-of-arrival (DoAs) and distance components. Building on these new representations, we employ a gridless approach for DoAs estimation using a von Mises distribution, and a coarse-to-fine grid search for distance estimation. We then develop a semi-gridless variational Bayesian (SG-VB) algorithm with efficient update rules that enables accurate channel reconstruction. Simulation results validate the effectiveness of the proposed SG-VB algorithm, demonstrating enhanced near-field channel reconstruction accuracy and superior estimation performance for both DoAs and distance components embedded in near-field channels."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:31Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21444","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21444","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21444","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marilet","familyName":"De Andrade","name":"De Andrade, Marilet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joachim","familyName":"Sachs","name":"Sachs, Joachim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas","familyName":"Haug","name":"Haug, Lucas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Egger","name":"Egger, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frank","familyName":"Dürr","name":"Dürr, Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Balázs","familyName":"Varga","name":"Varga, Balázs","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Janos","familyName":"Farkas","name":"Farkas, Janos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"György","familyName":"Miklós","name":"Miklós, György","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compensating the Packet Delay Variation for 6G Integrated with IEEE Time-Sensitive Networking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T23:46:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"6G is deemed as a key technology to support emerging applications with stringent requirements for highly dependable and timecritical communication. In this paper, we investigate 6G networks integrated with TSN and how to compensate for wireless stochastic behavior which involves a large intrinsic packet delay variation. We evaluate a 6G solution to reduce packet delay variation that is based on de-jittering. For this, we propose to use virtual timeslots for providing the required time-awareness. We discuss the benefits of the proposed solution while evaluating the impact of the timeslot size on the number of schedulable TSN streams."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the RTNS 2025 conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21444","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:23Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21418","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21418","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21418","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadsaleh","familyName":"Razmi","name":"Razmi, Mohammadsaleh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iman","familyName":"Shojaei","name":"Shojaei, Iman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Event-Driven On-Sensor Locomotion Mode Recognition Using a Shank-Mounted IMU with Embedded Machine Learning for Exoskeleton Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.9; C.3; J.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:45:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:11:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work presents a wearable human activity recognition (HAR) system that performs real-time inference directly inside a shank-mounted inertial measurement unit (IMU) to support low-latency control of a lower-limb exoskeleton. Unlike conventional approaches that continuously stream raw inertial data to a microcontroller for classification, the proposed system executes activity recognition at the sensor level using the embedded Machine Learning Core (MLC) of the STMicroelectronics LSM6DSV16X IMU, allowing the host microcontroller to remain in a low-power state and read only the recognized activity label from IMU registers. While the system generalizes to multiple human activities, this paper focuses on three representative locomotion modes - stance, level walking, and stair ascent - using data collected from adult participants. A lightweight decision-tree model was configured and deployed for on-sensor execution using ST MEMS Studio, enabling continuous operation without custom machine learning code on the microcontroller. During operation, the IMU asserts an interrupt when motion or a new classification is detected; the microcontroller wakes, reads the MLC output registers, and forwards the inferred mode to the exoskeleton controller. This interrupt-driven, on-sensor inference architecture reduces computation and communication overhead while preserving battery energy and improving robustness in distinguishing level walking from stair ascent for torque-assist control."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 6 figures. Sensor-level HAR using embedded IMU machine learning for wearable robotics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21418","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:56:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21405","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21405","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21405","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lewis J","familyName":"Miller","name":"Miller, Lewis J","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kathryn","familyName":"Grasha","name":"Grasha, Kathryn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Federrath","name":"Federrath, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The turbulence driving mode in NGC7793 and NGC1313"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:23:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:10:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1093/mnras/stag359","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present spatially resolved measurements of turbulence driving modes across entire extragalactic discs of NGC7793 and NGC1313, using Atacama Large Millimetre/submillimetre Array (ALMA) CO(J=2-1) observations at 13pc resolution. By applying a kernel-based analysis of density and velocity fluctuations, we map the turbulence driving parameter, b, which characterises the balance between solenoidal ($b\\sim0.3$) and compressive ($b\\sim1$) turbulent driving regimes. b is quantified as the ratio of the turbulent density fluctuations relative to the turbulent sonic Mach number, M. Both galaxies show predominantly solenoidal driving on average for the regions where we find valid results ($b\\geq 0.33(\\pm 0.05)^{+0.14}_{-0.10}$ in NGC7793; $b\\geq 0.24(\\pm 0.03)^{+0.10}_{-0.07}$ in NGC1313), noting that this is without including the influences of magnetic fields, making these measurements lower limits. We find substantial spatial variation of b, including localised regions of strongly compressive driving. NGC1313 exhibits higher turbulent Mach numbers and density dispersions than NGC7793, consistent with the disturbed morphology and recent satellite interaction in NGC1313. The turbulence in both NGC7793 and NGC1313 is supersonic ($3\\lesssim M\\lesssim 20$), and NGC1313 shows a radially decreasing trend of M with galactocentric radius. Radial trends indicate more solenoidal driving in the galaxy centres, potentially reflecting enhanced shear, and increasingly compressive modes in the outskirts. These results demonstrate that turbulence driving varies systematically with galactic environment and cannot be assumed uniform across discs. Our study applies a previously established method to larger scales and new data, linking local turbulence physics to global star formation regulation in galaxies, providing a new avenue for testing theoretical models with future integral field units and ALMA surveys."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 9 figures, 3 tables, accepted for publication by MNRAS (19/02/26), results presented at Stellar Origins 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21405","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:27Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:56:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21400","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21400","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21400","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teng Ee","familyName":"Yap","name":"Yap, Teng Ee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David J.","familyName":"Stevenson","name":"Stevenson, David J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Formation of Water-rich Giant Planet Satellites at Decretion Disk Ice Lines"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:11:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:09:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3847/psj/ae336a","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The volatile budgets of giant planet satellites are critical to unraveling the origin of their building blocks within the circumplanetary disks that hosted them. The Galilean moons Ganymede and Callisto, as well as the Saturnian moon Titan, are known to be anomalously water rich on the basis of their mean densities and interior models informed by gravity data from Galileo and Cassini, characterized by ice-to-rock ratios around unity. Here, we show that the water-ice sublimation line in a decreting circumplanetary disk lends itself to the formation of a water-rich solid reservoir, serving as a natural site for the birthplace of icy satellites. Fundamentally, this reflects how interior to the ice line, water vapor is advected outward, while beyond it, water ice drifts inward as pebbles. Using a semi-analytic model for dust and vapor evolution, we simulate vapor and ice accumulation at the ice line, showing that solids just beyond it achieve steady-state ice-to-rock ratios a factor of a few higher than elsewhere in the disk. For typical disk parameters, this ice buildup occurs within a timescale of a few thousand years. We propose this as a first-order process that explains, at least to some extent, the compositions of three aforementioned satellites. We explore the impact of uncertain turbulence parameters on our results, namely the turbulent Schmidt number and Shakura-Sunyaev alpha, before discussing them in the context of icy satellite D/H ratios. We conclude by evaluating alternative scenarios for explaining water-rich satellites, based on the conversion of CO to CH4, with water as a by-product."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21400","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:56:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21373","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21373","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21373","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takuma","familyName":"Nakamura","name":"Nakamura, Takuma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel J.","familyName":"Elson","name":"Elson, Daniel J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuta","familyName":"Wakayama","name":"Wakayama, Yuta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles A.","familyName":"McLemore","name":"McLemore, Charles A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tetsuya","familyName":"Hayashi","name":"Hayashi, Tetsuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikael","familyName":"Mazur","name":"Mazur, Mikael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Fontaine","name":"Fontaine, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franklyn","familyName":"Quinlan","name":"Quinlan, Franklyn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nazanin","familyName":"Hoghooghi","name":"Hoghooghi, Nazanin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fiber Link Stabilization with a Multicore Fiber Amplifier"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:06:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the use of separate cores of a multicore erbium-doped fiber amplifier (MC-EDFA) in a noise-canceled link for ultrastable optical frequency transfer. We demonstrate fractional frequency instability of $5\\times10^{-19}$ at 1000 s averaging time for the stabilized MC-EDFA alone and $1.4\\times10^{-18}$ at 1000 s averaging time when integrated with a 40 km-long 7-core spooled fiber. This study further establishes multicore fiber (MCF) networks as a promising platform for ultrastable frequency transfer, serving as an important step toward incorporating precision time and frequency distribution into future MCF communication infrastructures."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21373","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:40Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:55:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai Surya","familyName":"Duvvuri","name":"Duvvuri, Sai Surya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanakya","familyName":"Ekbote","name":"Ekbote, Chanakya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rachit","familyName":"Bansal","name":"Bansal, Rachit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishabh","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Rishabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devvrit","familyName":"Khatri","name":"Khatri, Devvrit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Brandfonbrener","name":"Brandfonbrener, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Inderjit","familyName":"Dhillon","name":"Dhillon, Inderjit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manzil","familyName":"Zaheer","name":"Zaheer, Manzil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Interleaved Head Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:05:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-Head Attention (MHA) is the core computational primitive underlying modern Large Language Models (LLMs). However, MHA suffers from a fundamental linear scaling limitation: $H$ attention heads produce exactly $H$ independent attention matrices, with no communication between heads during attention computation. This becomes problematic for multi-step reasoning, where correct answers depend on aggregating evidence from multiple parts of the context and composing latent token-to-token relations over a chain of intermediate inferences. To address this, we propose Interleaved Head Attention (IHA), which enables cross-head mixing by constructing $P$ pseudo-heads per head (typically $P=H$), where each pseudo query/key/value is a learned linear combination of all $H$ original queries, keys and values respectively. Interactions between pseudo-query and pseudo-key heads induce up to $P^2$ attention patterns per head with modest parameter overhead $\\mathcal{O}(H^2P)$. We provide theory showing improved efficiency in terms of number of parameters on the synthetic Polynomial task (IHA uses $Θ(\\sqrt{k}n^2)$ parameters vs. $Θ(kn^2)$ for MHA) and on the synthetic order-sensitive CPM-3 task (IHA uses $\\lceil\\sqrt{N_{\\max}}\\rceil$ heads vs. $N_{\\max}$ for MHA). On real-world benchmarks, IHA improves Multi-Key retrieval on RULER by 10-20% (4k-16k) and, after fine-tuning for reasoning on OpenThoughts, improves GSM8K by 5.8% and MATH-500 by 2.8% (Majority Vote) over full attention."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:37Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:55:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21293","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21293","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21293","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","