Yihang","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayuan","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Jiayuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahua","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jiahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinrong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sailang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Sailang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinfeng","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Jinfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaozu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yaozu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hekang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hekang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiujiang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qiujiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guo-Yi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Guo-Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Teleportation transition of surface codes on a superconducting quantum processor"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T19:00:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:02:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The topological surface code is a leading candidate for harnessing long-range entanglement to protect logical quantum information against errors, and teleportation of logical states is desirable for robust quantum information processing. Nevertheless, scaling up the surface code in quantum teleportation poses a formidable challenge to experiment. Here on a superconducting quantum processor with 125 qubits, we demonstrate the robust teleportation of topological rotated surface code prepared by a linear-depth unitary circuit, with code distances up to 7. We obtain the teleportation phase diagram by tuning the local entangling gates uniformly across a finite threshold. Furthermore, we show that the entangling threshold can be boosted by coherent qubit rotations that inject magic resources beyond the Clifford regime, restoring the duality symmetry of the topological phase, which serves as a guiding principle to minimize the entanglement resource. Our results shed light on simulating and leveraging topological quantum matter on quantum devices, and pave the way to the ultimate goal of distributed fault tolerant quantum computation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21293","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:42Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:53:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21278","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21278","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21278","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lixian","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Lixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Upton","name":"Upton, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoqi","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zhuoqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengman","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shengman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koustav","familyName":"Jana","name":"Jana, Koustav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peijing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Peijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesse","familyName":"Cirimelli-Low","name":"Cirimelli-Low, Jesse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thierry","familyName":"Tambe","name":"Tambe, Thierry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Guthaus","name":"Guthaus, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H. -S. Philip","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, H. -S. Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Heterogeneous Memory Design Exploration for AI Accelerators with a Gain Cell Memory Compiler"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T18:10:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:02:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As memory increasingly dominates system cost and energy, heterogeneous on-chip memory systems that combine technologies with complementary characteristics are becoming essential. Gain Cell RAM (GCRAM) offers higher density, lower power, and tunable retention, expanding the design space beyond conventional SRAM. To this end, we create an OpenGCRAM compiler supporting both SRAM and GCRAM. It generates macro-level designs and layouts for commercial CMOS processes and characterizes area, delay, and power across user-defined configurations. The tool enables systematic identification of optimal heterogeneous memory configurations for AI tasks under specified performance metrics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21278","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:53:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianneng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jianneng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre V.","familyName":"Morozov","name":"Morozov, Alexandre V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Neural network optimization strategies and the topography of the loss landscape"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"G.1.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T17:49:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:02:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural networks are trained by optimizing multi-dimensional sets of fitting parameters on non-convex loss landscapes. Low-loss regions of the landscapes correspond to the parameter sets that perform well on the training data. A key issue in machine learning is the performance of trained neural networks on previously unseen test data. Here, we investigate neural network training by stochastic gradient descent (SGD) - a non-convex global optimization algorithm which relies only on the gradient of the objective function. We contrast SGD solutions with those obtained via a non-stochastic quasi-Newton method, which utilizes curvature information to determine step direction and Golden Section Search to choose step size. We use several computational tools to investigate neural network parameters obtained by these two optimization methods, including kernel Principal Component Analysis and a novel, general-purpose algorithm for finding low-height paths between pairs of points on loss or energy landscapes, FourierPathFinder. We find that the choice of the optimizer profoundly affects the nature of the resulting solutions. SGD solutions tend to be separated by lower barriers than quasi-Newton solutions, even if both sets of solutions are regularized by early stopping to ensure adequate performance on test data. When allowed to fit extensively on the training data, quasi-Newton solutions occupy deeper minima on the loss landscapes that are not reached by SGD. These solutions are less generalizable to the test data however. Overall, SGD explores smooth basins of attraction, while quasi-Newton optimization is capable of finding deeper, more isolated minima that are more spread out in the parameter space. Our findings help understand both the topography of the loss landscapes and the fundamental role of landscape exploration strategies in creating robust, transferrable neural network models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages in the main text + 5 pages in the supplement. 6 figures + 1 table in the main text, 4 figures and 1 table in the supplement"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:53:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21272","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21272","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21272","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reuben","familyName":"Cohn-Gordon","name":"Cohn-Gordon, Reuben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Uroš","familyName":"Seljak","name":"Seljak, Uroš","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dries","familyName":"Sels","name":"Sels, Dries","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Counterdiabatic Hamiltonian Monte Carlo"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation (stat.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:56:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (HMC) is a state of the art method for sampling from distributions with differentiable densities, but can converge slowly when applied to challenging multimodal problems. Running HMC with a time varying Hamiltonian, in order to interpolate from an initial tractable distribution to the target of interest, can address this problem. In conjunction with a weighting scheme to eliminate bias, this can be viewed as a special case of Sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) sampling \\cite{doucet2001introduction}. However, this approach can be inefficient, since it requires slow change between the initial and final distribution. Inspired by \\cite{sels2017minimizing}, where a learned \\emph{counterdiabatic} term added to the Hamiltonian allows for efficient quantum state preparation, we propose \\emph{Counterdiabatic Hamiltonian Monte Carlo} (CHMC), which can be viewed as an SMC sampler with a more efficient kernel. We establish its relationship to recent proposals for accelerating gradient-based sampling with learned drift terms, and demonstrate on simple benchmark problems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21272","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:53:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21250","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21250","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21250","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Messan Médard","familyName":"Akouetegan","name":"Akouetegan, Messan Médard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isiaka","familyName":"Aremua","name":"Aremua, Isiaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahouton Norbert","familyName":"Hounkonnou","name":"Hounkonnou, Mahouton Norbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constructing Barut-Girardello coherent states for the isotonic oscillator in the DOOT approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-19T11:25:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we study the quantum system of the isotonic oscillator from the perspective of the diagonal operator ordering technique (DOOT). Within this framework, we construct the associated Barut-Girardello and Gazeau-Klauder coherent states. We examine their mathematical properties using reproducing kernels and compute the expectation values of observables that characterize the system and its relevant physical features. Further, we perform the quantization of main classical variables in the complex plane. Then, by exploring the thermal behavior of the physical system in the constructed coherent states, we analyze the properties of mixed states described by a canonical density operator. We also obtain the corresponding Glauber-Sudarshan P-representation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21250","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:37Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21244","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21244","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21244","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Roohi","name":"Roohi, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmad","familyName":"Shoja-Sani","name":"Shoja-Sani, Ahmad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Stefanov","name":"Stefanov, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics Constrained Neural Collision Operators for Variable Hard Sphere Surrogates and Ab Initio Angle Prediction in Direct Simulation Monte Carlo"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-15T03:58:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Direct Simulation Monte Carlo (DSMC) method is the gold standard for non-equilibrium rarefied gas dynamics, yet its computational cost can be prohibitive, especially for near-continuum regimes and high-fidelity \\emph{ab initio} potentials. This work develops a unified, physics-constrained neural-operator framework that accelerates DSMC while preserving physical invariants and stochasticity required for long-time kinetic simulations. First, we introduce a local neural collision kernel replacing the phenomenological Variable Hard Sphere (VHS) model. To overcome the variance suppression and artificial cooling inherent to purely deterministic regression surrogates, we augment inference with a physics-constrained stochastic layer. Controlled latent-noise injection restores thermal fluctuations, while cell-wise moment-matching strictly enforces momentum and kinetic-energy conservation. Remarkably, this operator exhibits zero-shot spatial and thermodynamic generalization: a model trained exclusively on 1D Couette flow accurately simulates a complex 2D lid-driven cavity, capturing high-order non-equilibrium moments without retraining.Second, to bypass the extreme cost of quantum-mechanical scattering, we develop a dedicated \\emph{ab initio} neural operator for the Jäger interaction potential. Trained via a \\emph{physics harvesting} strategy on large-scale collision pairs, it efficiently captures the high-energy scattering dynamics dominating hypersonic regimes. Validated on a Mach~10 rarefied argon flow over a cylinder, the framework reproduces transport behaviors and shock features with high fidelity, achieving an approximate 20\\% cost reduction relative to direct numerical integration."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21244","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:28Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21236","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21236","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21236","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Migliarini","name":"Migliarini, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Berat","familyName":"Ercevik","name":"Ercevik, Berat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oluwagbemike","familyName":"Olowe","name":"Olowe, Oluwagbemike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saira","familyName":"Fatima","name":"Fatima, Saira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh Anh","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Minh Anh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasu","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Vasu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashwinee","familyName":"Panda","name":"Panda, Ashwinee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"@GrokSet: multi-party Human-LLM Interactions in Social Media"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-11T12:42:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as active participants on public social media platforms, yet their behavior in these unconstrained social environments remains largely unstudied. Existing datasets, drawn primarily from private chat interfaces, lack the multi-party dynamics and public visibility crucial for understanding real-world performance. To address this gap, we introduce @GrokSet, a large-scale dataset of over 1 million tweets involving the @Grok LLM on X. Our analysis reveals a distinct functional shift: rather than serving as a general assistant, the LLM is frequently invoked as an authoritative arbiter in high-stakes, polarizing political debates. However, we observe a persistent engagement gap: despite this visibility, the model functions as a low-status utility, receiving significantly less social validation (likes, replies) than human peers. Finally, we find that this adversarial context exposes shallow alignment: users bypass safety filters not through complex jailbreaks, but through simple persona adoption and tone mirroring. We release @GrokSet as a critical resource for studying the intersection of AI agents and societal discourse."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21236","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:16Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21224","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21224","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21224","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuetao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuetao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xuliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinzhou","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xinzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Make Every Draft Count: Hidden State based Speculative Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-02T08:25:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speculative decoding has emerged as a pivotal technique to accelerate LLM inference by employing a lightweight draft model to generate candidate tokens that are subsequently verified by the target model in parallel. However, while this paradigm successfully increases the arithmetic intensity of memory-bound inference, it causes significant compute inefficiency: the majority of draft tokens fail verification and are discarded, resulting in waste of computation. Motivated by the goal of recollecting this wasted computation, we propose a novel system that transforms discarded drafts into reusable tokens. Our key insight is to perform auto-regressive prediction at the hidden states level and postpone the integrating token information after the hidden states generation, so the draft hidden states are not contaminated by incorrect tokens, enabling hidden state reuse. To implement such a system, first we introduce a draft model architecture based on auto-regressive hidden states, which preserves richer semantics than token-based drafters to facilitate draft repurposing. Second, we design an efficient token information injection mechanism that leverages our specialized draft model to construct high-quality draft token trees and enables resampling tokens from verification failures. Third, we eliminate the overhead hidden in our design to further maximize hardware utilization. We conducted extensive evaluations against various baselines, demonstrating up to a 3.3x speedup against standard speculative decoding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21224","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21221","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21221","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21221","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeju","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zeju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhou","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Latent Context Compilation: Distilling Long Context into Compact Portable Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-31T08:38:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient long-context LLM deployment is stalled by a dichotomy between amortized compression, which struggles with out-of-distribution generalization, and Test-Time Training, which incurs prohibitive synthetic data costs and requires modifying model weights, creating stateful parameters that complicate concurrent serving. We propose Latent Context Compilation, a framework that fundamentally shifts context processing from adaptation to compilation. By utilizing a disposable LoRA module as a compiler, we distill long contexts into compact buffer tokens -- stateless, portable memory artifacts that are plug-and-play compatible with frozen base models. Crucially, we introduce a self-aligned optimization strategy that eliminates the need for synthetic context-relevant QA pairs. By regularizing context reconstruction task with context-agnostic random queries, we force compressed tokens to reside within the model's existing instruction-following manifold. Experiments with Llama-3.1-8B demonstrate that Latent Context Compilation preserves fine-grained details and reasoning capabilities where prior methods falter, effectively decoupling memory density from model parameters even at a 16x compression ratio."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21221","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21212","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21212","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21212","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takato","familyName":"Yasuno","name":"Yasuno, Takato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Disaster Question Answering with LoRA Efficiency and Accurate End Position"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; K.4.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-28T01:53:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Natural disasters such as earthquakes, torrential rainfall, floods, and volcanic eruptions occur with extremely low frequency and affect limited geographic areas. When individuals face disaster situations, they often experience confusion and lack the domain-specific knowledge and experience necessary to determine appropriate responses and actions. While disaster information is continuously updated, even when utilizing RAG search and large language models for inquiries, obtaining relevant domain knowledge about natural disasters and experiences similar to one's specific situation is not guaranteed. When hallucinations are included in disaster question answering, artificial misinformation may spread and exacerbate confusion. This work introduces a disaster-focused question answering system based on Japanese disaster situations and response experiences. Utilizing the cl-tohoku/bert-base-japanese-v3 + Bi-LSTM + Enhanced Position Heads architecture with LoRA efficiency optimization, we achieved 70.4\\% End Position accuracy with only 5.7\\% of the total parameters (6.7M/117M). Experimental results demonstrate that the combination of Japanese BERT-base optimization and Bi-LSTM contextual understanding achieves accuracy levels suitable for real disaster response scenarios, attaining a 0.885 Span F1 score. Future challenges include: establishing natural disaster Q\\\u0026amp;A benchmark datasets, fine-tuning foundation models with disaster knowledge, developing lightweight and power-efficient edge AI Disaster Q\\\u0026amp;A applications for situations with insufficient power and communication during disasters, and addressing disaster knowledge base updates and continual learning capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21212","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":99,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2602%2A+AND+registered%3A%5B2026-02-25T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2026-02-26T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28GPU+OR+kernel+OR+accelerator+OR+distributed+OR+communication+OR+serving+OR+quantization+OR+compiler%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=1000"}}